const TRAVEL_TOWN_STAY: &str = "Book close to the middle of town and a street or two back from the main road: the station, the food and the evening are then walkable, and the rooms just off the front are quieter and cheaper. At the seaside that means a street back from the front rather than the front itself. Look at the map for a bar or a through road under the window, check the last bus or train if you will be out late, and book somewhere with free cancellation so you can move if the area is not what you hoped.";
static TRAVEL_CITY_AREAS: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("rio de janeiro", "Stay in Ipanema for the beach and the bars, or Santa Teresa for the old streets on the hill."),
    ("san francisco", "Stay in the Mission for food and the sun, North Beach for Italian and the wharf, or SoMa for the middle of it."),
    ("san sebastian", "Stay in the old town for pintxos on the doorstep, or Gros for the surf beach and quieter nights."),
    ("buenos aires", "Stay in Palermo for restaurants and going out, Recoleta for the grand streets, or San Telmo for the old quarter."),
    ("los angeles", "Stay in Santa Monica for the beach, Hollywood for the sights and grit, or Downtown for the arts and the bars."),
    ("mexico city", "Stay in Roma and Condesa for leafy streets and cafes, Centro for the sights, or Coyoacan for quiet."),
    ("copenhagen", "Stay in Norrebro for food and a local feel, Vesterbro for bars near the meatpacking district, or Indre By to be in the middle."),
    ("barcelona", "Stay in the Eixample for a well-connected, walkable base (keep an eye on your belongings), Gracia for a village feel, or El Born for tapas and narrow streets."),
    ("amsterdam", "Stay in the Jordaan for canals and quiet, De Pijp for the food market and locals, or the centre if you want everything on foot and do not mind noise."),
    ("stockholm", "Stay in Sodermalm for bars and views, Gamla Stan for the postcard and the crowds, or Ostermalm for somewhere smarter."),
    ("edinburgh", "Stay in the New Town for Georgian calm and everything on foot, the Old Town for the Royal Mile and tourists, or Leith for the food."),
    ("marrakech", "Stay in a riad inside the medina for the atmosphere, Gueliz for modern hotels and quiet, or the Kasbah for the sights."),
    ("singapore", "Stay in Chinatown for food and value, Marina Bay for the sights, or Orchard for shopping."),
    ("reykjavik", "Stay in 101 downtown to walk to everything, or Laugavegur for the shops and bars and a short walk to the harbour."),
    ("ljubljana", "Stay in the old town by the river for cafes and everything on foot, or Tivoli park side for green and quiet."),
    ("dubrovnik", "Stay in inside the walls for the postcard and the noise, or Lapad and Babin Kuk for beaches and calmer nights and a bus into town."),
    ("santorini", "Stay in Imerovigli or Pyrgos for the view without the crowds, or Oia for the sunset and the people who come to see it."),
    ("cape town", "Stay in the city bowl for the restaurants and the mountain, Camps Bay for the beach, or Stellenbosch if you want the wine farms."),
    ("budapest", "Stay in District V for the centre, District VII for the ruin bars and the noise, or the Buda side for quiet and the views."),
    ("helsinki", "Stay in Kallio for bars and cheaper rooms, the Design District for the centre on foot, or Kamppi for the transport hub."),
    ("florence", "Stay in Santa Croce for the centre and a lively square, Oltrarno for artisans and quiet, or around Santa Maria Novella for the station."),
    ("istanbul", "Stay in Sultanahmet for the sights on foot and quiet nights, Beyoglu for going out, or Kadikoy on the Asian side for food and locals."),
    ("new york", "Stay in Midtown for the sights and the noise, the Lower East Side for food and bars, or Brooklyn for cheaper rooms and 20 minutes on the train."),
    ("valencia", "Stay in the Carmen for the old town and bars, or the beach end for sand and the tram."),
    ("seville", "Stay in Santa Cruz for narrow streets and the Alcazar, Triana for ceramics and locals, or the Alameda for bars."),
    ("chicago", "Stay in River North to walk to the lake and the galleries, Wicker Park for bars, or the Loop for the trains."),
    ("bangkok", "Stay in Sukhumvit for hotels and food, the riverside for views and boats, or Khao San for the backpacker end."),
    ("toronto", "Stay in King West for bars and the centre, Kensington Market for food and a local feel, or the Distillery District for cobbles."),
    ("tallinn", "Stay in the old town for the walls and the square, or Kalamaja for wooden houses and cafes and a short walk in."),
    ("granada", "Stay in the Albaicin for the views and the old streets, or the centre near the cathedral for tapas on the doorstep."),
    ("london", "Stay in Bloomsbury to walk to most of it, the South Bank for the river and theatres, or Shoreditch for bars and a late night."),
    ("madrid", "Stay in La Latina for tapas on the doorstep, Malasana for going out, or Salamanca for somewhere smart and quiet."),
    ("lisbon", "Stay in Alfama for the old streets and the views but steep and noisy, Baixa and Chiado to be in the middle of it, or Principe Real for quiet and smart."),
    ("berlin", "Stay in Mitte for the centre and the museums, Kreuzberg for nightlife and grit, or Prenzlauer Berg for cafes and families."),
    ("vienna", "Stay in the Innere Stadt to walk to the palaces and concerts, Leopoldstadt for the Prater and cheaper rooms, or Neubau for small shops and cafes."),
    ("prague", "Stay in Vinohrady for leafy streets and local restaurants, Mala Strana for quiet and pretty, or the Old Town for the middle of it and the noise."),
    ("dublin", "Stay in Portobello for quiet and locals, St Stephen's Green to be central, or Temple Bar for the pubs and the noise that comes with them."),
    ("venice", "Stay in Cannaregio for local life and canals, Dorsoduro for art and quiet, or San Marco for the middle of it and the day trippers."),
    ("naples", "Stay in Chiaia for the seafront and somewhere smart, the Centro Storico for food and chaos, or Vomero for the hill and a calm night."),
    ("athens", "Stay in Koukaki for the museum and local tavernas, Plaka for under the Acropolis, or Exarchia if you want the edgier side."),
    ("sydney", "Stay in Surry Hills for cafes and bars, Circular Quay for the harbour on the doorstep, or Bondi for the beach."),
    ("boston", "Stay in Back Bay for the shopping and the river, Beacon Hill for the postcard streets, or the North End for Italian food."),
    ("krakow", "Stay in the Old Town for the square and the sights on foot, or Kazimierz for bars, food and a local feel."),
    ("munich", "Stay in the Altstadt for the beer halls and the sights, Schwabing for a student feel and the English Garden, or near the station for day trips."),
    ("zurich", "Stay in the Niederdorf for the old town on foot, or the west side near the river for bars and cheaper rooms."),
    ("havana", "Stay in Habana Vieja for the old streets on foot, or Vedado for 1950s hotels and a local evening."),
    ("paris", "Stay in Le Marais for the centre and cafes, Saint-Germain for the classic postcard, or Canal Saint-Martin for cheaper nights and a younger crowd."),
    ("milan", "Stay in Brera for chic and central, the Navigli for canals and nightlife, or Porta Romana for quieter streets."),
    ("porto", "Stay in Ribeira for the river and the crowds, Cedofeita for shops and cafes, or Bonfim for local streets and cheaper rooms."),
    ("tokyo", "Stay in Shinjuku for the transport and the nightlife, Asakusa for the old town and quiet, or Shibuya if you want to be in the middle of it."),
    ("kyoto", "Stay in Kawaramachi for the centre and dinner, Gion for the old streets, or Arashiyama for quiet and the bamboo."),
    ("dubai", "Stay in Dubai Marina for the beach and towers, Downtown for the Burj and the malls, or Deira for the souks and lower prices."),
    ("hanoi", "Stay in the old quarter for the street food and the lake on foot, or the French quarter for quieter nights."),
    ("rome", "Stay in Monti for the Colosseum on foot and quiet nights, Trastevere for restaurants and cobbles, or Prati for the Vatican and a calmer evening."),
    ("oslo", "Stay in Grunerlokka for cafes and the river, the centre for the harbour and the station, or Majorstuen for quiet."),
    ("bath", "Stay in the centre by the Abbey to walk to the baths and the shops, or the Royal Crescent end for Georgian streets and quiet."),
];
static TRAVEL_FAMILY_VERDICTS: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("rio de janeiro", "Rio is wonderful with children in the right places: the beaches, the cable car up Sugar Loaf and the botanical garden are the easy days. Take advice on which areas to walk and which to take a taxi through. Before swimming, get local advice and follow beach flags and lifeguard directions."),
    ("san francisco", "San Francisco is a good family city: the cable cars, the sea lions at Pier 39 and the science museum fill three days, and it is all close. The hills are steep and the summer fog makes it colder than people expect."),
    ("san sebastian", "San Sebastian is a seaside city built for families: the beach curls round the bay in the middle of town, the promenade is flat, and the old town tapas bars take children at the early end of the evening. It is a city for eating and swimming rather than for sights."),
    ("buenos aires", "Buenos Aires is a big, late city with children: the parks and the zoo in Palermo are the easy days, and the buses and taxis are cheap. Dinner starts at nine or later, so plan an afternoon snack that counts as their meal."),
    ("los angeles", "Los Angeles is a driving city, so plan days out rather than wanders: the beach, the studios and the theme parks are each a half day with a car. Without one it is hard work with children."),
    ("mexico city", "Mexico City is huge and rewarding with children: the parks, the anthropology museum and the boats at Xochimilco fill days, and the metro is cheap. It is high up, so take the first day slowly, and eat where it is busy."),
    ("copenhagen", "Copenhagen is about as good as it gets for small children: Tivoli, the Little Mermaid on a flat seafront walk, and the harbour buses for the price of a metro ticket. It is flat, and prams go everywhere."),
    ("amsterdam", "Amsterdam is easy with children: everything is close, the trams take a pram, and NEMO and the science museum are built for them. The bikes are the thing to watch - keep on the pavement side and hold hands at the crossings."),
    ("barcelona", "Barcelona is one of the easiest cities in Europe with children: the beach is on the metro, the Gothic Quarter is flat, and the Boqueria is a free hour of noise and fruit. The Sagrada Familia sells out, so book weeks ahead."),
    ("cape town", "Cape Town is excellent with children if you have a car: the beaches, the cable car, the penguins at Boulders and the aquarium fill a week. Plan drives in daylight and stay on main roads where possible. For a township visit, go with a responsible, reliable tour guide."),
    ("dubrovnik", "Dubrovnik is beautiful and hard with a pram: the old town is all steps and the walls are a long, hot walk with no shade. Go at opening time or after five, and take the cable car for the view instead of walking the walls."),
    ("edinburgh", "Edinburgh is a good family city if you pace the hills: the castle is a climb but there is a lift up from the esplanade, the museum is free and built for children, and the Royal Mile is short. In August the festival makes it crowded and expensive."),
    ("ljubljana", "Ljubljana is a small, gentle city with children: the centre is car-free, the river has cafes on it, and the castle funicular saves the climb. It is a good two-day stop on the way to the lakes or the coast."),
    ("marrakech", "Marrakech is a lot with small children: the souks are hot, crowded and full of scooters, and the food is not always what they will eat. The gardens, the horse-drawn calèche rides and a pool at the hotel make it work."),
    ("reykjavik", "Reykjavik is a good family base if you drive: the swimming pools are the thing, heated and outdoor and everywhere, and the whales, the glaciers and the golden circle are day trips from it. It is expensive, so an apartment helps."),
    ("santorini", "Santorini is a honeymoon island more than a family one: the villages are steps and cliff paths with no shade, and the beaches are black stones. With children a resort with a pool on a flatter island works far better."),
    ("singapore", "Singapore is one of the easiest cities in Asia with children: it is safe, the metro is step-free and air-conditioned, and the zoo, the aquarium and the gardens fill days. It is expensive, and the heat puts the outdoor plans in the morning."),
    ("stockholm", "Stockholm is lovely with children in summer: the ferries between the islands, the Vasa ship and the open-air museum at Skansen, and prams go everywhere. Winter is dark and cold, so go between May and September."),
    ("brussels", "Brussels is underrated with children: the Grand Place, the comic strip museum and the Atomium are close, and the waffles do a lot of the work. Two days is enough, and it is an easy stop on a longer trip."),
    ("budapest", "Budapest is good with children who like water: Margaret Island has pools, bikes and a musical fountain. Check each bath's age rules before booking with children. The trams and metro are cheap and frequent."),
    ("florence", "Florence with children is one big sight a day and a lot of ice cream: the Duomo climb suits older ones and the Boboli gardens have space to run. The Uffizi is a hard sell under ten, so take them to the Palazzo Vecchio instead."),
    ("helsinki", "Helsinki is calm and easy with children: the ferries to the islands, the zoo on its own island, and playgrounds everywhere in a small, walkable centre. It is expensive, and the winter dark is the reason to go between May and September."),
    ("istanbul", "Istanbul is good with children who can walk: the ferries are the best value day out in the city, and the tram between Sultanahmet and the bazaar takes a pram. The bazaars are crowded and pushy, so go early in the day."),
    ("new york", "New York is great with children who can keep going: the parks, the Natural History Museum and the Staten Island ferry are cheap days out. Most subway stations have no lift, so plan the stroller around buses and taxis."),
    ("valencia", "Valencia is a good family city that most people skip: the City of Arts and Sciences has an aquarium and a science museum built for children, and the old town is flat. The beach is a tram ride from the centre."),
    ("bangkok", "Bangkok is hot and loud and better with children than people expect: the river boats are the day out, the malls have play areas and aquariums, and taxis are cheap. Traffic is the enemy, so plan around the skytrain and the boats."),
    ("chicago", "Chicago is a very good family city: the museums on the lakefront, the beach in summer, and the trains are easy with a stroller. The winter wind off the lake is the reason to go between May and September."),
    ("granada", "Granada is worth it with children for the Alhambra alone - book months ahead and go at opening time, because there is little shade. The city is hilly and the tapas come free with a drink, which makes dinner easy and early."),
    ("seville", "Seville is good with children from March to May: the Alcazar has gardens to run in, the Plaza de Espana has rowing boats, and the tapas are cheap and served early enough for them. July and August are too hot to sightsee with children."),
    ("tallinn", "Tallinn is a good short family break: the old town is small and walkable, the towers and walls are climbable, and the ferries to Helsinki take two hours. Two days is plenty, and it is cheap by western European standards."),
    ("toronto", "Toronto is a good, manageable family city: the islands by ferry, the aquarium, the science centre and the hockey museum fill days, and the subway takes a stroller. It is clean and English-speaking, which makes the first day easy."),
    ("athens", "Athens is fine with older children and hard work with a toddler: the Acropolis is a stony climb with little shade and the pavements are narrow. Go at opening time, give the afternoon to the National Garden, and take taxis rather than walk the long blocks."),
    ("berlin", "Berlin works well with children and costs less than most of western Europe: the zoo is one of the best anywhere, the Technikmuseum has trains and planes to climb on, and the parks have playgrounds. It is spread out, so buy a day travel ticket."),
    ("boston", "Boston is a good family city: the Freedom Trail is a walk with stories in it, the harbour and the science museum fill a day, and the T takes a stroller. It is compact and walkable, which is the whole point of it with children."),
    ("dublin", "Dublin is a two-day city with children: the Natural History Museum, the Phoenix Park and the zoo sit close together and cost little. The Guinness tour is not one for them, so split up or skip it."),
    ("havana", "Havana is a city of old cars, music and heat, and children tend to love the first two: the Malecon, the squares and the museums are cheap. Take the heat seriously and carry water, and expect the food to be plainer than the city suggests."),
    ("krakow", "Krakow is a good value family city: the main square is flat and car-free, the dragon under the castle breathes fire every few minutes, and the salt mine is a half day out. The camps nearby are not for young children."),
    ("lisbon", "Lisbon is good with children if you take the trams and the funicular rather than walk the hills: the castle, the aquarium and Belem fill two easy days. The pavements are slippery when wet, and the hills are real."),
    ("london", "London is one of the best cities in the world with children: free museums, the parks, and a ride on the top deck of a bus is a day out on its own. The tube is not step-free everywhere, so check the station before planning a pram route."),
    ("madrid", "Madrid is good with children and cheap to eat: the Retiro has a lake with rowing boats and a playground, and the museums are free in the evenings. Dinner starts late, so plan a proper late-afternoon snack."),
    ("munich", "Munich is easy and safe with children: the English Garden, the Deutsches Museum and the beer gardens, where children are welcome and there is usually a playground. The trains out to the lakes and the castles are part of the trip."),
    ("naples", "Naples is loud and fast and children tend to love it: the pizza is cheap, the seafront is a flat walk, and Pompeii and Herculaneum are a short train away. Traffic is the thing to watch and the pavements are narrow."),
    ("prague", "Prague is good value with children: the castle, the river and the old town are close together, the trams are cheap, and the toy museum is a good wet-afternoon stop. The cobbles are hard on a pram."),
    ("sydney", "Sydney is a great family city: the beaches, the ferry to Manly, the zoo on the harbour and the parks, most of it free or cheap. It is spread out, so plan each day around one beach or one ferry."),
    ("venice", "Venice with children is a day of boats and bridges: the water bus is the day out, St Mark's square has pigeons and space, and there are no cars to worry about. A pram is the problem - bridges with steps come every hundred metres, so a carrier is better."),
    ("vienna", "Vienna is quietly excellent with children: the zoo at Schonbrunn, the Prater's old ferris wheel and a playground in every park, with a metro that takes a pram everywhere. The cafes take children, and lunch is cheap at midday."),
    ("zurich", "Zurich is clean, safe and expensive with children: the lake boats, the zoo and the outdoor swimming baths are the days out, and the trams take a pram. A Swiss pass and a supermarket lunch keep the cost down."),
    ("dubai", "Dubai is easy for families and hot for most of the year: the malls have indoor play, aquariums and a ski slope, so it works in August when nothing outdoors does. Anything outdoors belongs to the morning, and the good things need booking."),
    ("hanoi", "Hanoi is exciting and full-on with children: the scooters make crossing the road an event, and the old quarter is loud. The water puppet theatre and the parks are the easy wins, and a hotel with a pool earns its money."),
    ("kyoto", "Kyoto is quiet and beautiful and hard work with small children: the temples ask for quiet and the buses are packed. The monkey park and the bamboo grove suit them, and a stroller is more trouble than it is worth on temple steps."),
    ("milan", "Milan is a short family stop rather than a holiday: the Duomo, the castle park and the science museum are close, and the trams take a pram. Two days, then on to the lakes."),
    ("paris", "Paris is good for families if you pick one thing a day: the Luxembourg gardens have boats and a playground, and the big museums are free for under-18s in the EU. Lunch is the easy meal out - restaurants are friendlier at midday than at eight."),
    ("porto", "Porto is a good short family break: the riverfront is flat, the boats and the old tram are the day out, and the port cellars are a walk across the bridge. The climb back up from the river is steep."),
    ("tokyo", "Tokyo is easier with children than people expect: the trains are safe and punctual, lifts and toilets are everywhere, and there is a small park on every block. The trains are packed at rush hour, so plan around eight in the morning and six at night."),
    ("bath", "Bath is a good day out rather than a week: the Roman Baths run a family trail with an audio guide for children, and the baths, the abbey and the river are all within a mile. It is fine with a pram in the centre and steep on the hills out of it."),
    ("oslo", "Oslo is small, safe and expensive: the ferries, the opera house roof you can walk on, and the sculpture park are the easy days out, and the trams take a pram. A week of self-catering takes the edge off the prices."),
    ("rome", "Rome works with children if you keep the days short: the Colosseum and the Forum are one morning, the Pantheon and a gelato another, and Rome's public drinking fountains (nasoni) and water houses give you free drinking water. The cobbles are hard on a pram and the summer heat is real, so spring or autumn."),
];
const SUPERMARKET_CHEAPEST_TAIL: &str = " It is one of the cheapest places to do a full shop, though the range of brands is smaller, so anything specific may need a second stop.";
const SUPERMARKET_MIDDLE_TAIL: &str = " The basics are price-matched to the discounters, so it is the brands in the trolley that make the difference: a basket of own-brand staples will be cheaper at Aldi or Lidl.";
const SUPERMARKET_DEAR_TAIL: &str = " For a cheaper basket on the same list, the discounters save around a fifth and Asda or Tesco most of the rest.";
static SUPERMARKETS: &[shopping_comparisons::Store] = &[
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Aldi", aliases: &["aldi"], rank: 0, description: "the cheapest of the big names", reason: "own-brand staples run roughly a fifth less than at Tesco or Sainsbury's, on a smaller range with few named brands", contrast: "has the cheaper staples and a smaller range" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Lidl", aliases: &["lidl"], rank: 0, description: "one of the two cheapest of the big names", reason: "own-brand staples run roughly a fifth less than at Tesco or Sainsbury's, with a strong bakery and few named brands", contrast: "is level on price and stronger on the bakery" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Farmfoods", aliases: &["farmfoods"], rank: 0, description: "cheap for frozen food", reason: "the frozen and branded boxes are cheap and the fresh food is thin", contrast: "is cheaper on frozen and thinner on fresh" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Asda", aliases: &["asda"], rank: 1, description: "usually the cheapest of the big four", reason: "a like-for-like basket comes out cheapest at Asda, and it price-matches Aldi and Lidl on the basics", contrast: "price-matches Aldi on the basics and runs more brands" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Iceland", aliases: &["iceland"], rank: 1, description: "cheap for frozen food", reason: "frozen and ready meals are cheap and everything else is dearer", contrast: "is cheaper on frozen and dearer on the rest" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Morrisons", aliases: &["morrisons", "morrison"], rank: 2, description: "mid-priced", reason: "it sits with Tesco and Sainsbury's, and is often the cheapest of the big four on fresh meat and fish", contrast: "is close on price with better fresh meat and fish" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Tesco", aliases: &["tesco", "tescos"], rank: 2, description: "mid-priced", reason: "its Aldi Price Match holds the basics at Aldi's price, and the rest of the basket costs more", contrast: "holds the basics at Aldi's price and costs more for the rest" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Sainsbury's", aliases: &["sainsburys", "sainsbury's", "sainsbury"], rank: 2, description: "mid-priced like Tesco", reason: "the basics are price-matched to Aldi and the brands are what cost more", contrast: "matches the basics and costs more for the brands" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Ocado", aliases: &["ocado"], rank: 3, description: "M&S food delivered", reason: "it is priced like M&S rather than like a discounter", contrast: "is priced like M&S" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "The Co-op", aliases: &["co-op", "co op", "coop", "co operative", "cooperative"], rank: 3, description: "a convenience shop", reason: "it is dearer than the big four on almost everything, so it is a top-up shop", contrast: "is dearer, which is what the convenience buys" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "Waitrose", aliases: &["waitrose"], rank: 4, description: "one of the two dearest", reason: "it runs around 10-20% more than Tesco on the same basket, and the fresh food is better", contrast: "costs more and is better on fresh food" },
    shopping_comparisons::Store { name: "M&S", aliases: &["marks and spencer", "marks spencer", "m and s", "m&s", "marks & spencer"], rank: 4, description: "the dearest of the big names", reason: "it runs around 10-20% more than Tesco on the same basket, and the ready meals are why people go", contrast: "costs more, and the ready meals are why people go" },
];
static SHOPPING_APPLIANCE_VALUE: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["air fryer", "air fryers", "airfryer"], "An air fryer is a small oven that blows hot air fast: chips, roast veg and salmon in 10 to 15 minutes with a spoon of oil. Worth it for one or two people who hate heating a big oven; skip it for four, since a 4-litre basket is one tray. £50 to £80 is plenty."),
    (&["slow cooker", "slow cookers"], "A slow cooker is worth it if you are out all day: load it before work and cheap cuts are tender by dinner. Skip it if you want anything crisp or fast. A £30 one cooks the same as a £100 one."),
    (&["pressure cooker", "instant pot", "pressure cookers"], "A pressure cooker is worth it if you cook beans, stews or tough meat from scratch: a beef stew is 40 minutes instead of two hours, and dried beans are 25. Skip it for anything quick or crisp, and know that it is a big pan to store. A 6-litre one suits most kitchens."),
    (&["bread maker", "bread machine", "bread makers"], "A bread maker is not worth it for most people: skip it unless you eat a loaf a day, since it takes three to four hours for a small loaf with a soft crust, and a £15 tin in a hot oven makes better bread. It earns its space only if you want the dough made overnight with no hands."),
    (&["dishwasher", "dishwashers"], "A dishwasher is worth it for three or more people: it uses less water than washing up by hand and saves about half an hour a day. Skip it if you live alone and cook little, since filling it takes a week. Check the noise (under 45dB) if the kitchen is also the living room, and a 45cm slimline model fits a small kitchen."),
    (&["heat pump dryer", "heat pump dryers", "heat pump tumble dryer", "heat pump tumble drier", "heat pump drier", "heat pump condensing dryer"], "A heat pump dryer is worth it if you dry two or more loads a week: it uses about half the electricity of a vented one, roughly 25p a cycle against 60p, so 150 to 200 pounds more up front pays back in about two years. It runs cooler, so a load takes an hour longer, and the fluff filter and condenser want clearing often. Skip it if you dry rarely, if the room is cold, or if you have nowhere to empty the water."),
    (&["tumble dryer", "tumble dryers"], "A tumble dryer is worth it if you have no outside space or a house full of laundry: a heat-pump dryer costs more (£350 up) but uses about half the electricity of a vented one. Skip it if you have a garden and an airer: it is the most expensive way to dry clothes."),
    (&["smart thermostat", "smart thermostats", "heating thermostat", "nest thermostat", "hive thermostat", "tado", "thermostatic radiator valve", "thermostatic radiator valves", "trv", "trvs"], "A smart thermostat is worth it if the house is empty in the day: about £50 to £100 a year off a gas bill, so £150 to £250 fitted pays back in two to three years. Skip it if someone is home all day, or if the heating is already set to come on only when you are in: turning the flow temperature down and putting thermostatic valves on the radiators gets much of the saving for a fraction of the price."),
    (&["water filter jug", "water filter jugs", "filter jug", "brita", "water filter"], "A water filter changes the taste and takes out the chlorine, and a plumbed-in one under the sink is the same job done once instead of every jug: neither softens hard water nor takes out much else. Worth it if your tap water tastes of chlorine and you drink it cold all day, at about £20 for the jug and £4 a month for cartridges. A jug of tap water left in the fridge overnight does most of the same for nothing, so try that first."),
    (&["humidifier", "humidifiers"], "A humidifier is worth it in one dry room, usually a bedroom with the heating on and the windows shut, if you wake with a dry throat or nosebleeds, or have a cold. Skip it in a room that has damp on the walls or condensation on the glass, because adding moisture makes that worse, and clean it every week: a dirty humidifier sprays mould into the air. £30 to £60 for a cool-mist one."),
    (&["mattress topper", "mattress toppers", "bed topper"], "A mattress topper is worth it if the mattress is sound but too firm, or you want it softer: 5cm of memory foam or a wool topper costs £60 to £120 and changes the feel for a fraction of a new mattress. It will not fix a mattress that sags, is over about eight years old or gives you backache, and a topper on a bad mattress is £100 spent on the wrong thing."),
    (&["water softener", "water softeners", "water softening"], "A water softener is worth it in a hard water area if scale is a daily nuisance: kettles, shower screens and the washing machine stop furring up, and the boiler keeps its efficiency. It is about £1,000 to £2,000 fitted plus £100 to £150 a year in salt, so it is a family and hard-water answer rather than a flat one. A filter jug does not soften water, and a magnetic or electronic conditioner does not take the calcium out."),
    (&["robot mower", "robot mowers", "robot lawn mower", "robotic mower"], "A robot mower is worth it for a flat, open lawn with no narrow gaps: it cuts a little every day, so the grass stays tidy and there is no grass to collect. It costs £500 to £1,500 and most need a boundary wire laid round the edge. Skip it for a small lawn, a steep slope, or one full of bulbs and toys."),
    (&["video doorbell", "video doorbells", "ring doorbell", "doorbell camera"], "A video doorbell is worth it if parcels go missing or you cannot hear the door from where you work: £50 to £120, and stored clips usually need a subscription of £2 to £4 a month unless you take a local-storage model. Wired is more reliable than battery. Point it at your own path and door rather than the pavement, and if you rent, ask before drilling."),
    (&["fan heater", "fan heaters", "electric heater", "electric heaters", "oil filled radiator", "convector heater", "space heater", "halogen heater"], "A fan heater is worth it for one room for twenty minutes, not for a whole evening or a whole house: cheap to buy at £20 to £40 and dear to run, since 2kW is about 60p an hour on current electricity prices. For a room you sit in, an oil-filled radiator is silent and holds the heat after it clicks off, and heating the person beats heating the room: a throw, a hot water bottle and warm socks are free after the first winter."),
    (&["fan", "fans", "electric fan", "tower fan", "pedestal fan", "desk fan", "floor fan", "standing fan", "cooling fan"], "A fan does not cool the air, it moves it, and moving air over your skin is enough to feel a few degrees cooler, so it is worth it for sleeping in a hot room: a tower or pedestal fan at £25 to £60 is much quieter than a cheap one, and it costs a penny or two an hour to run. Put it in the window at night to pull cool air in, and skip portable air conditioning unless the room is also humid, since that is the expensive way round."),
    (&["robot vacuum", "robot hoover", "robot vacuums"], "A robot vacuum is worth it as a daily top-up on hard floors and one level, not as a deep clean: skip it if the floor has stairs, thick rugs or a trail of cables and toys, since it will not get them and you still empty it. Best value in a house with a pet that sheds."),
    (&["dehumidifier", "dehumidifiers"], "A dehumidifier is worth it in a flat that steams up and grows black mould on the window frames: a 10 to 12 litre one dries washing indoors and stops the mould, which costs more to fix than the machine. Skip it if the damp is a leak or a broken gutter, since that needs fixing at the source."),
    (&["coffee machine", "bean to cup", "coffee machines"], "A coffee machine is worth it if you drink two coffees a day at home: a £150 bean-to-cup pays for itself in about three months against buying them out. Skip it if you have one coffee a day: a £30 filter machine with fresh ground coffee tastes better than a cheap pod machine, and the pods are the expensive part."),
    (&["stand mixer", "kitchenaid", "stand mixers"], "A stand mixer is worth it if you bake most weeks: it creams butter and kneads dough for you and lasts decades. Skip it if you bake a few times a year: a £15 hand mixer does the same job and lives in a drawer."),
    (&["meal kit", "meal kits", "recipe box", "hello fresh", "gousto"], "A meal kit is worth it for the decision taken away, not the food: about £5 a portion and no waste. Skip it if you already cook, since the same dinner is £1.50 a portion from a supermarket. Try two boxes and cancel."),
    (&["kindle", "e reader", "ereader", "e-reader"], "A Kindle is worth it if you read in bed or travel: a Paperwhite is readable in the dark, holds hundreds of books and the battery lasts weeks. Skip it if you mostly read at home: library ebooks through Libby or BorrowBox are free on a phone, and paper is better for anything with maps or photos. Buy last year's model, not the newest."),
    (&["noise cancelling headphones", "noise cancelling headphone", "noise cancelling"], "Noise cancelling headphones are worth it if you commute by train, plane or sit in a loud office: over-ear ones cut the engine noise that earbuds let through. About £70 to £120 is the sweet spot, and last year's model sounds the same as the new one for half the price. Skip them for a quiet room or for cycling, where hearing traffic matters."),
    (&["fitness tracker", "fitness trackers", "fitbit"], "A fitness tracker is worth it for steps and sleep, not for the heart-rate number: a £25 band counts steps and sleep well, and a watch ten times the price adds mostly a screen and GPS. Skip it if you already walk enough and know it, since most people stop looking after a month."),
    (&["smart watch", "smart watches", "apple watch"], "A smart watch is worth it if you want notifications, contactless payment and a run tracker on your wrist without getting your phone out. Skip it if you only want steps, where a £25 band does the same. An iPhone pairs best with an Apple Watch, an Android with a Wear OS watch or a Garmin."),
    (&["standing desk", "standing desks", "sit stand desk"], "A standing desk is worth it if you sit for eight hours and your back aches, and the useful part is changing position rather than standing all day: a £25 riser on the desk you have does most of it. Skip the motorised ones unless you know you will raise them, and stand for 20 minutes at a time rather than the afternoon."),
    (&["weighted blanket", "weighted blankets"], "A weighted blanket is worth it if the feeling of weight settles you: about 10% of your body weight is the usual guide, and it helps some people fall asleep and does nothing for others. Skip it for a toddler, or for anyone who could not push it off, and try one under a duvet before spending £60."),
    (&["steam cleaner", "steam cleaners", "steam mop"], "A steam cleaner is worth it for hard floors, tiles and grout, and the bathroom: it lifts grease with no chemicals and the water is free after the machine. Skip it on laminate, unsealed wood and anywhere the moisture can get under the floor, and skip it for carpets, where a hire machine once a year is cheaper than owning one."),
    (&["a cleaner", "the cleaner", "my cleaner", "our cleaner", "cleaners worth", "cleaning lady", "cleaning service", "housekeeper"], "A cleaner is worth it if two hours back at the weekend matter more than £15 to £20 an hour: two hours a fortnight covers a flat. Go through an agency with insurance, agree in writing what is included and be in the first time. Skip it while you are paying off a debt."),
    (&["food processor", "food processors", "kitchen machine"], "A food processor is worth it if you chop a lot of onions or make pastry: it does in a minute what a knife takes ten. Skip it if you cook simply, since it is heavy to wash up for one job. A £40 one chops as well as a £200 one."),
    (&["air purifier", "air purifiers"], "An air purifier is worth it in a bedroom if you have hay fever, a cat or a sealed window: a HEPA filter on low all night changes how you sleep. Skip it for damp, which needs a dehumidifier, and check the replacement filters are cheap before you buy."),
    (&["heated airer", "heated clothes airer", "heated drying rack", "heated clothes horse"], "A heated airer is worth it if you dry clothes indoors in winter: about 6p an hour against 50p or more for a tumble dryer, and it does not shrink anything. Skip it if you have a garden and dry outside in summer, and buy the larger winged one so a full wash fits on it. A cover cuts the drying time by half."),
    (&["electric blanket", "electric blankets", "heated blanket"], "An electric blanket is worth it for a cold bedroom: 20 minutes before bed costs pennies and beats heating the room all evening. Skip it if it would be on all night, since a hot water bottle does the same for nothing, and replace one over ten years old."),
    (&["microwave", "microwaves"], "A microwave is worth it for reheating, porridge, scrambled eggs and defrosting, not for cooking a meal from scratch: skip it if you want to roast or bake, since an oven does that better. A basic 800W one is enough; pay more for a bigger turntable and easier controls, not for modes nobody uses."),
    (&["rice cooker", "rice cookers"], "A rice cooker is worth it if rice is a daily thing: it times it, keeps it warm for an hour and leaves the hob free, and a 40 pound one cooks as well as a 200 pound one. Skip it if you eat rice twice a month, since a pan with a lid is the same rice in 12 minutes."),
    (&["soup maker", "soup makers"], "A soup maker is worth it if you make soup most weeks and want one jug and no pan to watch: it cooks and blends in about 20 minutes. Skip it if you like soup with chunks in it, or cook for four, because most jugs hold 1.6 litres and the blade takes everything smooth."),
    (&["sous vide", "sous vide stick", "sous vide machine", "sous vide cooker"], "Sous vide is worth it if you cook steak, chicken breast or eggs to a temperature and do not mind waiting: a 60 pound stick in a lidded pan holds the water steady for an hour or more, and nothing overcooks. Skip it if dinner has to be on the table in 20 minutes, where a thermometer and a hot pan get most of the way."),
    (&["dehydrator", "dehydrators", "food dehydrator"], "A dehydrator is worth it if you dry things often: herbs, apple rings, jerky or vegetables for stock, in 4 to 12 hours at 40 to 60C with no oven on. Skip it if that is a twice-a-year job, since an oven on its lowest setting with the door ajar dries a tray at a time."),
    (&["juicer", "juicers"], "A juicer is worth it only if you drink juice most mornings: it separates the pulp in seconds, and cleaning the mesh takes five minutes every time. Skip it if you would rather eat the fruit, which keeps the fibre the juice leaves behind."),
    (&["yogurt maker", "yoghurt maker", "yogurt makers"], "A yogurt maker is worth it if you get through a tub a week: a litre of milk makes a litre of yogurt for about 60p, and it holds the warmth steady for 8 to 12 hours. Skip it if you eat yogurt now and then, since a flask and a warm cupboard do the same job."),
    (&["ice cream maker", "ice cream makers"], "An ice cream maker is worth it if you make it more than a few times a summer: the bowl has to be frozen overnight, so it is never a last-minute thing, and a 40 pound one with a pre-frozen bowl is fine. Skip it if you would use it twice, where a loaf tin and a fork every half hour gets close."),
    (&["contact grill", "panini press", "sandwich press", "george foreman", "health grill"], "A contact grill is worth it if you toast sandwiches or cook chops and vegetables for one or two: it heats both sides at once and the fat runs off. Skip it if you cook for four, since the plates take two sandwiches at a time, and skip it if you have a frying pan and a lid."),
    (&["electric steamer", "steam cooker", "food steamer"], "An electric steamer is worth it if you steam vegetables, dumplings or fish often and like setting a timer and walking away: the tiers hold a whole dinner over one ring. Skip it if a pan with a colander and a lid already does it, which for most people it does."),
    (&["electric whisk", "hand whisk", "hand mixer", "electric hand whisk"], "An electric hand whisk is worth it for anyone who creams butter or whips cream even a few times a month: about 15 pounds, a minute instead of ten with a fork, and it lives in a drawer. Skip it only if you bake twice a year."),
    (&["mandoline", "mandolines", "mandoline slicer"], "A mandoline is worth it if you slice a lot of vegetables thin and even - gratins, slaw, pickles - and you will use the hand guard every single time. Skip it if you cook for one: a sharp knife is safer and nearly as quick."),
    (&["spiralizer", "spiralizers", "spiraliser"], "A spiralizer is worth it only if you really eat courgette or carrot noodles: a 10 pound hand-held one does it in a minute and the counter-top ones add nothing. Skip it if you would use it once, where a julienne peeler does the same job and fits in a drawer."),
    (&["soda stream", "sodastream", "soda maker", "sparkling water maker"], "A soda maker is worth it if you drink sparkling water every day: a gas cylinder makes about 60 litres for 10 pounds to swap, so it pays for itself in a few months and saves carrying bottles. Skip it if you drink it now and then, or would mostly use the flavour syrups, which cost more than supermarket bottles."),
    (&["vacuum sealer", "vacuum sealers", "food sealer", "food sealers"], "A vacuum sealer is worth it if you buy meat in bulk or freeze portions: it stops freezer burn and the bags can be boiled, so a batch of chilli reheats in the bag. Skip it if you freeze little, since a freezer bag with the air pressed out does most of it."),
];
static SHOPPING_CHOICE_VALUE: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["gym membership", "gym memberships", "join a gym", "joining a gym", "gym worth", "should i go to the gym", "should i go to gym", "shall i go to the gym", "should i go back to the gym", "should i go to the gym again", "should i bother going to the gym", "should i bother with the gym", "should i bother with a gym", "should i start going to the gym", "is it worth going to the gym", "worth going to the gym", "any point going to the gym", "should i go to the gym or"], "A gym is worth it if the door is within ten minutes of you and you will go twice a week: the walk decides it, not the equipment. Skip it if the nearest one is a drive away, and pay monthly rather than a year up front. A £20 leisure-centre one you use beats a £60 one you dread."),
    (&["heat pump dryer", "heat pump tumble dryer", "heat pump drier", "heat pump tumble drier", "heat pump dryers", "heat pump condensing dryer"], "A heat pump dryer is worth it if you dry two or more loads a week: the cycle uses about half the electricity of a vented one, roughly 25p against 60p, so it pays back its extra 150 to 200 pounds in about two years. It runs cooler and slower, so a load takes two hours and more, and it needs the fluff filter and condenser cleared often. Skip it if you dry rarely or have nowhere for the waste water, and never buy one without a heat pump if the room is cold."),
    (&["heat pump", "heat pumps"], "If you are replacing your boiler, ask a qualified heat pump installer to assess your home's heat loss, insulation and heating system. They can explain whether the radiators or insulation need an upgrade. Compare installation quotes and estimated running costs for your home, and check current Ofgem Boiler Upgrade Scheme eligibility if you live in England or Wales."),
    (&["electric car", "electric cars", "ev "], "An electric car is worth it if you can charge at home and do under about 200 miles a week: home charging is roughly 3p a mile against 15p for petrol, and there is almost no servicing. Skip it if you cannot charge at home, or if you regularly do long motorway runs, where public charging costs nearly as much as fuel."),
    (&["smart meter", "smart meters"], "A smart meter is worth having: your supplier fits one free, and the in-home display shows what the kettle and the heating cost as you use them. Ask for a second-generation (SMETS2) one so it keeps working if you switch supplier. It will not cut the bill on its own."),
    (&["water meter", "water meters"], "A water meter usually saves money if there are more bedrooms than people: the rule of thumb is that you pay less on a meter when there are fewer of you than rooms. Check the bill estimate first. If you requested the meter voluntarily in England or Wales, you can usually return to your previous billing method within 12 months. Ask your water company what applies to you, especially if a meter was already in use or metering is compulsory."),
    (&["season ticket", "season tickets"], "A season ticket pays off at about three days a week: below that, the flexible or weekly options are usually cheaper. Work out the yearly cost against 40 weeks of daily returns before you commit, and check whether a part-time or flexi season covers the days you actually travel."),
];
