const PET_CAT_CHILDREN: &str = "A cat and a child get on fine with three rules: never leave a baby or a toddler alone in a room with the cat, give the cat somewhere up high and a room it can leave by, and show the child one finger down the back and to leave the cat alone while it is eating or in its basket. The old story that a cat smothers a baby is not true - a cat goes to warmth, so keep it out of the cot, and shut the bedroom and the pram against it while the baby sleeps. A swat is nearly always fear rather than nastiness: a cat that swats a child it used to accept should see the vet, because pain is often behind a change. Wash any scratch with soap and water, and a deep bite on a hand wants looking at.";
const PET_LOSS_REPLY: &str = "I'm sorry, that's one of the hardest days. Nothing has to be decided today: the vet can arrange cremation or a home burial, and you can ask for a paw print or a lock of fur to keep. Be gentle with yourself for a while; it does get easier.";
const PET_LOSS_PREY_REPLY: &str = "That is the hunting instinct rather than anything wrong with the animal. Clear what it brings in away in gloves, bin or bury it where nothing else can get at it, and wash your hands after. Hunters pick up worms from what they catch, so keep the worming up to date, and a quick-release collar with a bell plus keeping it in from dusk until morning cuts how often it happens.";
static PET_SCRATCH_REPLIES: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("door", "A pet that scratches the door wants through, so the scratching is the way it has learned to ask. Do not open it while the paw is on the door: wait for a pause of a few seconds, then open it, and it learns the pause is what works. Hanging a bell on the door and teaching a touch of the nose to it gives a cat a quieter way to ask, and a dog with a shut door between him and you usually wants company more than the room: put his bed on the side of the door you are on, and leave the door open once he is settled."),
    ("floor", "Scratching the floor or the bedding before lying down is normal: dogs and cats turn and scrape to flatten a spot and leave their scent, and it is not a fault. It is worth answering if it is new or constant: give him a soft bed of his own so he has something to turn round in, check the room is not too warm, and if a dog is scraping hard at hard flooring his claws may be catching, so have them clipped. A cat that scrapes at the carpet by the door is marking, and a mat or a vertical post in that spot takes the behaviour."),
    ("furniture", "Put a tall scratching post right beside the corner she uses and make the sofa less appealing with a throw or double-sided tape for a few weeks: cats scratch to mark territory and to shed the outer claw, so the post has to be tall enough to stretch up and steady enough not to wobble. A dog scratching at the sofa or the cushions is usually digging up a nest, so give him a blanket to scrabble at and a walk before the evening, and if it is one spot in particular put something solid in front of it."),
    ("furniture-dog", "A dog scrabbling at the sofa or the cushions is usually digging itself a nest, so give him a blanket or an old duvet of his own to scrabble at and a walk before the evening. If it is one spot he keeps going at, put something solid in front of it for a fortnight and praise him when he uses his own bed instead. Chewing the furniture is a different problem with a different answer, so say if that is what is happening."),
    ("skin", "A pet scratching itself sore is a skin question, not a behaviour one: fleas are the first thing to rule out, then a food or pollen allergy, and a cat over-grooming a bald patch can be mites or stress. Have the vet look before the skin is broken, because an open scratch gets infected. In the meantime a flea treatment from the vet rather than the supermarket, no shampoo unless the vet names one, and a cone if he will not leave a wound alone."),
    ("", "Give them something that is theirs to scratch and put it where they already do it: a tall, steady post for a cat, and a blanket or a bit of matting for a dog. Cats scratch to mark and to shed claw, so a post beside the sofa stops the sofa being the spot, and a dog scraping at the floor or the bedding before lying down is just making a nest. Clipped claws and a walk before the evening take the rest of it, and scratching that has suddenly become constant, or is leaving the skin raw, is the vet's."),
];
