const BOOK_SIMILAR: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("normal people", "If you liked *Normal People*, *Conversations with Friends* is the same author on the same ground, and *Exciting Times* by Naoise Dolan has the same cool, sharp voice about love and money. For warmth too, *The Rachel Incident* by Caroline O'Donoghue."),
    ("the midnight library", "*The Humans* by the same author is the closest, and *A Man Called Ove* by Fredrik Backman has the same kind of kindness in it. *Eleanor Oliphant Is Completely Fine* if you want another odd person finding their way."),
    ("gone girl", "Try *The Silent Patient* by Alex Michaelides: short chapters, an unreliable narrator and a twist you won't see coming. *The Girl on the Train* by Paula Hawkins is the other obvious next step, and *Sharp Objects* is the same author as Gone Girl and nastier."),
    ("the thursday murder club", "The next one is *The Man Who Died Twice* by Richard Osman, the same four friends again. For a different author, *The Marlow Murder Club* by Robert Thorogood is close in tone, or *The Silent Patient* if you want a darker twist."),
    ("the martian", "Try *Project Hail Mary* by Andy Weir, the same author: one man solving one problem at a time, funnier than The Martian. For something bigger but just as engineering-minded, *Seveneves* by Neal Stephenson."),
    ("project hail mary", "*The Martian* is the same author and the same kind of puzzle-solving, and *Children of Time* by Adrian Tchaikovsky is the step up: bigger ideas, still easy to read."),
    ("harry potter", "*His Dark Materials* by Philip Pullman is the next step: magic, a child at the centre of it and better writing. *Percy Jackson* by Rick Riordan is easier and funnier, and *The Worst Witch* suits a younger reader."),
    ("klara and the sun", "*Never Let Me Go* is the same author on the same question, and quieter. *Machines Like Me* by Ian McEwan is the other one about a machine in a family."),
    ("never let me go", "*Klara and the Sun* and *The Remains of the Day* are the same author, and both are about somebody who only sees the truth too late. *The Road* by Cormac McCarthy if you want the same quiet grief."),
    ("the secret history", "*If We Were Villains* by M. L. Rio is the closest thing to it, and *The Likeness* by Tana French has the same closed group of clever friends. *The Goldfinch* is the same author and longer."),
    ("where the crawdads sing", "*The Great Alone* by Kristin Hannah has the same wild setting and the same lone girl, and *The Marsh King's Daughter* by Karen Dionne is the darker version."),
    ("eleanor oliphant is completely fine", "*A Man Called Ove* by Fredrik Backman is the same lonely person, warmer, and *The Rosie Project* by Graeme Simsion is funnier. Both are easy to read."),
    ("the song of achilles", "*Circe* is the same author and just as good, and *The Silence of the Girls* by Pat Barker tells the same war from the women's side."),
    ("the silent patient", "*The Girl on the Train* by Paula Hawkins and *The Guest List* by Lucy Foley both keep the twist hidden, and both are quick. *Gone Girl* if you want the coldest narrator of the lot."),
    ("a little life", "*Shuggie Bain* by Douglas Stuart is the same weight of sadness and just as well written, and *The Goldfinch* by Donna Tartt is the other long one people finish and then sit still."),
    ("the book thief", "*All the Light We Cannot See* by Anthony Doerr is the closest in feeling, and *The Boy in the Striped Pyjamas* is easier if it is for a younger reader."),
    ("lessons in chemistry", "*Remarkably Bright Creatures* by Shelby Van Pelt has the same warmth and a woman who carries on her own, and *Eleanor Oliphant Is Completely Fine* the same odd, funny voice."),
    ("the seven husbands of evelyn hugo", "*Daisy Jones and the Six* by Taylor Jenkins Reid is the same author and the same glamour with a dark edge, and *City of Girls* by Elizabeth Gilbert is the other long, warm life story."),
    ("conversations with friends", "*Normal People* is the same author and easier, and *Exciting Times* by Naoise Dolan has the same clever young people making a mess of it."),
    ("it ends with us", "*Me Before You* by Jojo Moyes is the one everyone else starts with, and *The Last Letter from Your Lover* is the same kind of love story with a hard centre."),
    ("the hunger games", "*Divergent* by Veronica Roth is the closest to it, and *The Giver* by Lois Lowry is shorter and stranger. For a grown-up version, *The Handmaid's Tale*."),
    ("the girl with the dragon tattoo", "*The Girl on the Train* moves faster, and *The Silence of the Lambs* is the colder one. *Sharp Objects* by Gillian Flynn if you want a family full of secrets."),
    ("the glass hotel", "*Station Eleven* is the same author and the better one to read first: the same quiet collapse and the same people holding on. *Cloud Atlas* by David Mitchell is the other one that moves between times like this."),
    ("station eleven", "*The Glass Hotel* is the same author, and *The Road* by Cormac McCarthy is the harsher, shorter version of the same world. *Severance* by Ling Ma is the funnier one."),
    ("verity", "The same kind of twist, done colder: *The Housemaid* by Freida McFadden, or *The Last Mrs Parrish* by Liv Constantine. *The Silent Patient* if you have not read it yet."),
    ("the housemaid", "*Verity* by Colleen Hoover is the closest and the fastest, and *The Last Mrs Parrish* by Liv Constantine has the same servant-in-the-house trick with more bite."),
    ("fourth wing", "*A Court of Thorns and Roses* by Sarah J. Maas is the same author-on-romance-and-war shape and the usual next step, and *Throne of Glass* is the same author, longer."),
    ("the da vinci code", "*Angels and Demons* is the same author and the same puzzle chase. For the real thing, *The Name of the Rose* by Umberto Eco is the same story with more history."),
    ("to kill a mockingbird", "*Of Mice and Men* is shorter and just as much about who gets a fair go, and *The Book Thief* is the other one about a child watching a town fail."),
    ("of mice and men", "*To Kill a Mockingbird* is the other short one about fairness, and *The Grapes of Wrath* is the same author and the same journey, much longer."),
    ("lord of the flies", "*The Hunger Games* does the same thing with a plot, and *The Graveyard Book* by Neil Gaiman suits a reader who wants it darker but shorter."),
    ("life of pi", "*The Old Man and the Sea* is the short one with the same sea and the same stubborn man, and *The Alchemist* is the other fable people read next."),
    ("the kite runner", "*A Thousand Splendid Suns* is the same author and better, and *And the Mountains Echoed* is the third one. *The Bookseller of Kabul* if you want the true version."),
    ("the alchemist", "*Life of Pi* has the same fable feel with a story that holds, and *Siddhartha* by Hermann Hesse is the short one behind it."),
    ("percy jackson", "*Magnus Chase* by Rick Riordan is the same author doing Norse myth, *Artemis Fowl* by Eoin Colfer is cleverer and funnier, and *Nevermoor* by Jessica Townsend is the safest pick for a younger reader."),
    ("the maze runner", "*The Hunger Games* by Suzanne Collins if you have not read it, *Divergent* by Veronica Roth next, and *Scythe* by Neal Shusterman when you want the same idea with more thinking in it."),
    ("divergent", "*The Hunger Games* is the one to read first if you skipped it, *Legend* by Marie Lu is the closest in shape, and *The Darkest Minds* by Alexandra Bracken has the same runaways-with-powers feel."),
    ("twilight", "*The Mortal Instruments* by Cassandra Clare is the next step for most readers, *Vampire Academy* by Richelle Mead is lighter and funnier, and *Crave* by Tracy Wolff is the newer, sillier version."),
    ("lord of the rings", "*The Hobbit* first if you have not read it. After that *The Name of the Wind* by Patrick Rothfuss for the prose, or *A Wizard of Earthsea* by Ursula K. Le Guin if you want the whole thing in 200 pages."),
    ("the hobbit", "*The Lord of the Rings* is the sequel and the reason to read it. *The Chronicles of Narnia* if you want shorter and lighter, or *A Wizard of Earthsea* for a quieter kind of magic."),
    ("game of thrones", "*The Blade Itself* by Joe Abercrombie for the same grim humour with fewer characters to keep track of, *The Name of the Wind* for one voice telling its own story, or *The Priory of the Orange Tree* if you want dragons in a single volume."),
    ("dune", "*Foundation* by Isaac Asimov for the politics across centuries, *Hyperion* by Dan Simmons for the strangeness, and *Children of Time* by Adrian Tchaikovsky for the biology."),
    ("1984", "*Animal Farm* by the same author first, because it is one evening's reading and the same argument. Then *Brave New World* by Aldous Huxley, and *Fahrenheit 451* by Ray Bradbury for the shortest of the three."),
    ("animal farm", "*1984* by the same author is the longer version of the same warning, then *Brave New World* by Aldous Huxley, or *Fahrenheit 451* by Ray Bradbury if you want it short."),
    ("brave new world", "*1984* by George Orwell is the pair everyone reads next, *Fahrenheit 451* by Ray Bradbury is shorter, and *The Handmaid's Tale* by Margaret Atwood is the one that still feels closest to now."),
    ("the handmaid's tale", "*The Testaments* by Margaret Atwood is the sequel from 2019 and answers what happened. *Vox* by Christina Dalcher is the same idea done faster, and *Red Clocks* by Leni Zumas is the quieter one."),
    ("pride and prejudice", "*Persuasion* is the next Austen - shorter and sadder. *Jane Eyre* by Charlotte Bronte for the gothic version, or *Bridget Jones's Diary* for the modern retelling."),
    ("the great gatsby", "*The Sun Also Rises* by Hemingway for the same lost generation, *Mrs Dalloway* by Virginia Woolf for a party that holds a whole life, or *Rules of Civility* by Amor Towles for the modern version."),
    ("diary of a wimpy kid", "*Tom Gates* by Liz Pichon is the same diary shape with more doodles, *Big Nate* by Lincoln Peirce is the school one, and *Captain Underpants* by Dav Pilkey if they want sillier."),
    ("alex rider", "*Cherub* by Robert Muchamore is the closest: teenagers trained as agents, more brutal than it looks. *Young Bond* by Charlie Higson for the origin story, or *Artemis Fowl* if they would rather be the genius."),
    ("jacqueline wilson", "*Cathy Cassidy* next for the same warm, short chapters, *The Suitcase Kid* or *Double Act* if they want another Wilson, or *Once* by Morris Gleitzman when they are ready for something harder."),
    ("agatha christie", "*The Thursday Murder Club* by Richard Osman for the modern cosy version, *Strong Poison* by Dorothy L. Sayers for the classic one, or *Magpie Murders* by Anthony Horowitz, which is a Christie puzzle inside a Christie puzzle."),
    ("stephen king", "*11.22.63* is his best if the storytelling was the draw, *The Shining* for the proper horror, and *Heart-Shaped Box* by Joe Hill - his son - for the same voice."),
    ("the fault in our stars", "*Eleanor & Park* by Rainbow Rowell is the closest in feeling, *Me and Earl and the Dying Girl* by Jesse Andrews if you want it funny as well as sad, and *Five Feet Apart* by Rachael Lippincott for the same story again."),
    ("the hate u give", "*Dear Martin* by Nic Stone is the closest next read, *The Poet X* by Elizabeth Acevedo if the voice is what you liked, and *Long Way Down* by Jason Reynolds, which is one elevator ride in verse."),
    ("wonder", "*Out of My Mind* by Sharon M. Draper, *Fish in a Tree* by Lynda Mullaly Hunt and *The Goldfish Boy* by Lisa Thompson all do the same thing: a child nobody expects much from, telling it themselves."),
    ("the catcher in the rye", "*The Perks of Being a Wallflower* by Stephen Chbosky is the closest in voice, *Looking for Alaska* by John Green for the same boy at boarding school, and *The Bell Jar* by Sylvia Plath when you want the darker one."),
    ("little women", "*Anne of Green Gables* by L. M. Montgomery, *Pride and Prejudice* for the sisters and the wit, or *The Railway Children* by E. Nesbit for the family doing without."),
    ("anne of green gables", "*Emily of New Moon* is the same author's other girl and most people who love Anne love her too. *Little Women* next, or *Pollyanna* for the same bright stubbornness."),
    ("the secret garden", "*The Railway Children* by E. Nesbit, *Anne of Green Gables* for another child sent somewhere she does not want to be, or *Tom's Midnight Garden* by Philippa Pearce."),
    ("narnia", "*The Hobbit* is the obvious next one, *A Wizard of Earthsea* by Ursula K. Le Guin for the quieter magic, and *His Dark Materials* by Philip Pullman when they are a year or two older."),
    ("his dark materials", "*The Chronicles of Narnia* for the other world through a door, *A Wizard of Earthsea* for the magic with a price, and *The Lie Tree* by Frances Hardinge for the historical version."),
    ("ready player one", "*Snow Crash* by Neal Stephenson is the book it borrows from, *Ender's Game* by Orson Scott Card is the training-school one, and *The Martian* if the problem-solving was the best part."),
];
