const PASTA_KINDS_REPLY: &str = "Pasta comes down to six families, and the family decides the job: long strands (spaghetti, linguine and tagliatelle) for a ragu, a carbonara or a butter sauce the strands can carry; tubes (penne and rigatoni) for a baked dish, and a thick sauce that fills the tube; twists and shells (fusilli, farfalle and conchiglie) for a sauce with bits in it that needs something to cling to, and pasta salad; tiny shapes (orzo and stelline) for soup, and a salad that should not be all bulk; sheets (lasagne) for a bake, layered instead of tossed and filled (ravioli and tortellini) for a meal on its own with butter or a light sauce. The time goes by the shape as well: 8 to 9 minutes for thin strands, 10 to 11 for spaghetti and penne, 11 to 13 for rigatoni and tagliatelle, and 3 to 4 for the filled ones. Fresh pasta is 2 to 3 minutes, not the packet time.";
