const GLASSES_NAMING_WORDS: &[&str] = &["glasses", "specs", "spectacles", "sunglasses", "frame", "frames", "arm", "arms", "temple", "temples", "lens", "lenses", "rim", "rims", "bridge"];
const GLASSES_GLUE: &str = "Superglue is the stopgap, not the repair: a dab on a clean, dry break holds a plastic arm for a few weeks, but it will not take the strain of putting them on and it clouds a lens if it creeps. The lasting fix is an optician - a frame repair or your own lenses in a new frame is £20 to £50, often free if they sold you the glasses, and most do it while you wait. Do not use it on a metal hinge or on the bridge: those need a screw or a solder, and glue there makes the proper repair harder.";
const GLASSES_REPAIR: &str = "Take them to an optician first: a frame repair or a new frame with your lenses usually costs £20 to £50, many do it while you wait, and it is often free if the glasses came from them. A dab of superglue holds a plastic arm for a few weeks if you are stuck, but it will not take the strain of putting them on, so treat it as a stopgap. If the frame has snapped at the bridge or the lenses are scratched, a new pair is usually better value, and home contents insurance sometimes covers accidental damage.";
const WARDROBE_ADVICE: &str = "Buy the size you are now, not the one you were: two pairs of trousers and three tops that fit at the shoulder and the waist cover most of a week, and a straight cut or a little stretch forgives more than a fitted one. Measure yourself once with a tape over what you wear underneath and buy to those numbers, because the label varies by shop by as much as two inches - try the two nearest sizes and keep the one that sits right. Restock one thing at a time, in colours that go with what you already have, and don't buy a whole wardrobe until your weight has been steady for a couple of months.";
