const DRINK_DIFFERENCES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["cappuccino", "latte"], "A latte is the milkier of the two: a lot of steamed milk with a thin foam on top, in a bigger cup. A cappuccino is a third coffee, a third milk and a third foam, in a smaller cup, so it is drier to drink and tastes more of the coffee."),
    (&["flat white", "latte"], "A flat white is smaller and stronger: a double shot under a thin, glossy layer of milk. A latte is mostly milk. Order the flat white for the coffee and the latte for something mild and long."),
    (&["cappuccino", "flat white"], "Both are small and strong. The flat white has thinner, glossier milk and no foam cap; the cappuccino is a third foam, so the first sip is dry and there is less milk under it."),
    (&["americano", "espresso"], "An americano is an espresso with hot water added: the same coffee, a longer drink, and much less intense. An espresso is the 25 to 30ml shot on its own, with a thick crema."),
    (&["champagne", "prosecco"], "Champagne is fermented a second time in the bottle, so it tastes of brioche and toast with a sharper acidity; prosecco is made in a tank, so it is fruitier, lighter and cheaper. Both are dry, and prosecco is the easier one to drink."),
    (&["ale", "lager"], "Lager is fermented cold with a bottom yeast, so it is clean, crisp and lightly bitter. An ale is fermented warm, so it tastes fruitier and more bitter from the hops."),
    (&["ipa", "lager"], "An IPA is far hoppier - bitter, citrusy or piney, with a long finish - and usually stronger. A lager is clean and light, and the point of it is that it tastes of very little."),
    (&["cider", "lager"], "Cider is fermented apple juice, so it tastes of fruit and is often sweeter; beer is brewed from grain, so it tastes of hops and malt. Dry cider is the one closest to a lager in weight."),
    (&["red wine", "white wine"], "Red is fermented with the grape skins, which gives it the colour and the tannin that dries your mouth; white has no skins, so it is lighter in body and tastes of citrus, apple and stone fruit. Reds suit red meat, whites suit fish and chicken."),
    (&["green tea", "black tea", "earl grey"], "Green tea is unfermented leaf brewed at about 80C, so it is grassy and light. Black tea is fully oxidised and brewed boiling, so it is darker and maltier and takes milk; earl grey is black tea with bergamot oil on it."),
    (&["gin", "vodka"], "Gin is vodka with juniper and other botanicals distilled in, so it tastes of something. Vodka is distilled to taste of almost nothing, which is why it is the one you mix."),
    (&["whisky", "vodka"], "Whisky is grain spirit aged in wood, so it is coloured and tastes of vanilla, caramel or smoke; vodka is unaged and filtered to be neutral. Whisky is sipped and vodka is usually mixed."),
    (&["matcha", "green tea"], "Matcha is green tea leaf ground to a powder and whisked into the water, so you drink the whole leaf: it is stronger, creamier and more bitter than green tea, which is the leaf steeped and taken out."),
    (&["cappuccino", "flat white", "mocha"], "A mocha is a latte with chocolate in it: sweet, and coffee second. A flat white is the strongest of the milky coffees and a cappuccino is the foamiest."),
];
// bot/knowledge/seasoning.py: _SPICE_AMOUNTS and reply fences
const SEASONING_AMOUNTS: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["garlic", "cloves of garlic", "clove of garlic", "garlic cloves", "garlic clove"], "Two or three cloves for a curry, stew or sauce that serves four: a fat clove is about a teaspoon once crushed, so a teaspoon of the jarred paste stands in for one."),
    (&["garlic powder", "garlic granules"], "Half a level teaspoon of garlic powder for a curry for four, which stands in for a clove or two; granules are weaker, so use a whole teaspoon, and add either one with the liquid rather than at the end, because they need a minute to soften."),
    (&["black pepper", "ground pepper", "peppercorns", "pepper"], "Half a teaspoon of ground black pepper for a dish that serves four, stirred in at the end with the salt: pepper is aroma rather than heat, and it goes flat when it cooks for a long time, so a long stew wants it twice - once at the start and a little at the end."),
    (&["vanilla", "vanilla extract", "vanilla essence"], "A teaspoon of vanilla extract in a 20cm cake; essence is weaker, so use two, and vanilla paste about the same as the extract."),
    (&["cinnamon"], "A teaspoon or two in a 20cm sponge, and half a teaspoon on a bowl of porridge: start with one and taste, because an old jar is weaker than a new one."),
    (&["baking powder"], "Two level teaspoons per 200g of plain flour, which is what lifts a sponge. Self-raising flour already has it in, so add none there."),
    (&["bicarbonate of soda", "bicarbonate", "bicarb", "baking soda"], "Half a teaspoon per 200g of plain flour, with something acid - buttermilk, yogurt, lemon juice - to set it off; on its own it leaves a soapy taste."),
    (&["allspice", "ground allspice", "pimento", "pimento berries"], "A quarter teaspoon of ground allspice for a 20cm cake, or six berries in a jar of pickle: it tastes of clove, cinnamon and nutmeg at once, so a little stands in for all three."),
    (&["mixed spice", "pumpkin spice"], "Two teaspoons in a 20cm cake, or a teaspoon across a dozen buns: it is a blend, so it is gentler than any single spice in it."),
    (&["nutmeg"], "A quarter of a whole nut, grated, for a 20cm cake or a pan of rice pudding. Whole nutmeg keeps its flavour for years and ground loses it in months."),
    (&["ground ginger", "ginger"], "A teaspoon of ground ginger in a 20cm cake or a stir-fry for four; fresh ginger is about three times the weight for the same heat."),
    (&["garam masala"], "A teaspoon or two for a curry for four, stirred in at the very end with the heat off: it is a finishing blend, and cooked from the start it loses the scent that is the point of it."),
    (&["curry powder", "masala"], "A tablespoon of curry powder for a curry for four. Fry it in the oil for half a minute before the liquid goes in and it tastes twice as strong."),
    (&["chilli powder", "chili powder", "cayenne", "chilli flakes", "chili flakes"], "Half to one teaspoon for four, and taste before the second half: heat builds as it cooks, so it is easy to overshoot and hard to take back."),
    (&["chilli", "chili", "chillies", "chilis", "chile"], "One chilli for four is enough heat for most people: add a second at the end if you want more, and taking out the seeds and white pith halves the heat without losing the taste."),
    (&["cumin", "ground cumin", "cumin seeds"], "A teaspoon of ground cumin in a chilli or a curry for four, or a tablespoon of the seeds, which want thirty seconds in a dry hot pan first to wake them up."),
    (&["fresh coriander", "coriander leaves", "coriander to garnish", "coriander garnish"], "A small handful of fresh coriander for four, torn in at the end: the stems hold most of the flavour, so chop them into the sauce and keep the leaves for the top."),
    (&["coriander", "ground coriander", "coriander seeds"], "A teaspoon of ground coriander for a curry for four; the seeds toast in a dry pan in half a minute and grind to something fresher and more citrus than the jar."),
    (&["turmeric"], "Half a teaspoon is colour and a teaspoon is flavour for a dish that serves four; more than that turns bitter, which is why it is never heaped in."),
    (&["paprika", "smoked paprika"], "A teaspoon for four is enough for the colour and the smoke; it burns in a hot pan, so add it with the liquid rather than at the start."),
    (&["oregano", "dried oregano"], "A teaspoon of dried oregano for four, or a tablespoon of fresh leaves. Dried oregano wants ten minutes in the sauce to soften; fresh is stirred in at the end."),
    (&["thyme", "fresh thyme", "dried thyme", "thyme leaves"], "Three or four sprigs of fresh thyme for four, or a teaspoon of the dried leaves: the woody stems come out before serving, so tie them in a bundle if you want the job easy."),
    (&["rosemary", "fresh rosemary", "dried rosemary"], "One sprig for a roast for four, or a teaspoon of the needles chopped fine. It is strong and resinous, so it goes in at the start with the meat rather than at the end."),
    (&["sage", "fresh sage", "dried sage"], "Four or five leaves for four, or a teaspoon dried and crumbled: it is the strongest of the soft herbs and turns medicinal if it is doubled, so it is the one to under-do."),
    (&["bay leaf", "bay leaves", "bay"], "One leaf for a stew or a pan of rice for four, and take it out at the end: two is plenty in a big pot, and a broken leaf left in is what makes a stew taste of the cupboard."),
    (&["basil", "fresh basil", "dried basil"], "A handful of fresh leaves for four, torn in off the heat at the end, or a teaspoon of dried in the sauce while it cooks. Dried basil loses the scent that is the reason to use it."),
    (&["parsley", "fresh parsley", "dried parsley"], "Two tablespoons of chopped fresh parsley for four, stirred in at the end. Dried parsley is mostly for colour, so use twice as much and expect little taste from it."),
    (&["dill", "fresh dill"], "A tablespoon of chopped dill for four, in at the end: it is a soft herb and the scent goes with heat, so it is stirred through just before the pot comes off."),
    (&["mint", "fresh mint", "dried mint"], "A handful of leaves for four, torn rather than chopped, or a teaspoon of dried mint. In a yogurt sauce or a salad it wants to be the last thing that goes in."),
    (&["mixed herbs", "italian herbs", "herbes de provence", "dried herbs"], "A teaspoon of mixed dried herbs for four; a tablespoon if the pot is a big one and cooking for an hour or more, because the dried leaves give up their taste slowly."),
    (&["spice", "spices", "spice mix", "seasoning", "mixed herbs and spices"], "A tablespoon of a ground spice blend for a dish that serves four, and a tablespoon over a kilo of chicken: taste after the first spoonful, because blends vary more in heat than their labels let on."),
    (&["saffron"], "Three or four threads for a paella or a risotto for four, steeped in a spoonful of the hot stock first: any more than that and it starts to taste of the chemist's shelf."),
    (&["star anise", "star aniseed", "stars anise"], "One whole star anise for a bottle of mulled wine or a slow-cooked pork for four: it is liquorice-strong, and two would take over the whole pot."),
    (&["clove", "cloves", "ground clove", "ground cloves"], "A quarter teaspoon of ground cloves for a 20cm cake, or four to six whole ones in a bottle of mulled wine: it is strong, and more than that tastes medicinal."),
];
const SEASONING_NOT_A_DOSE: &[&str] = &["plant", "planting", "planted", "grow", "growing", "sow", "sowing", "good for", "bad for", "healthy", "safe", "allergy", "allergic", "ate", "eaten", "dog", "cat", "puppy", "kitten", "baby", "toddler", "pregnan", "out of date", "gone off", "mouldy", "price", "prices", "water", "sun", "sunlight", "soil", "compost", "fertiliser", "fertilizer", "plant food", "in a pot", "in the garden", "cost", "buy", "cheapest", "substitute", "instead of", "swap", "each", "servings", "portions", "buns", "rolls", "batches", "a crowd", "how much is", "how much are", "how much for", "jar", "jars", "in a bulb", "in one bulb", "a bulb of", "bulb of", "in my coffee", "in coffee", "in my tea", "in tea", "too much", "how much is too much", "limit", "a day", "per day", "con carne", "bell pepper", "bell peppers", "red pepper", "green pepper", "yellow pepper", "orange pepper", "sweet pepper", "sweet peppers", "jalapeno", "jalapenos", "scotch bonnet", "romano", "padron"];
const SEASONING_MULLED_WINE: &str = "Four to six whole cloves, a cinnamon stick snapped in two and a star anise in a bottle of red: warm it gently for twenty minutes without letting it boil, and taste before you add any sugar.";
const SEASONING_LATTE: &str = "A quarter to half a teaspoon for a mug, whisked into the coffee with the milk and a little sugar: it is mostly cinnamon, so it needs the sugar to taste of anything.";
static SCIENCE_NAMED_MOONS: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("jupiter", "Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto are the four worth knowing - the ones Galileo saw in 1610 - and Europa is the one with an ocean under the ice."),
    ("saturn", "Titan is the one worth knowing - bigger than Mercury, with lakes of methane - then Enceladus, Rhea, Iapetus and Mimas."),
    ("mars", "Phobos and Deimos, both small lumps of rock no more than twenty-odd kilometres across."),
    ("uranus", "Titania, Oberon, Umbriel, Ariel and Miranda, all named after Shakespeare and Pope characters."),
    ("neptune", "Triton, the big one, which orbits backwards and is probably a captured Kuiper-belt object."),
];
static SCIENCE_TALL_BUILDINGS: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["shard"], "The Shard in London is 310 m to the tip of its spire, 95 storeys, finished in 2012 - the tallest building in the United Kingdom and, for four years, in western Europe. Its floor plate is glass on all sides and the tower leans in as it rises, so the top storeys are smaller than the bottom ones."),
    (&["burj khalifa", "burj"], "The Burj Khalifa in Dubai is 828 m tall with 163 floors. It was completed in 2010 and is the tallest completed building in the world in CTBUH's 2026 list. Its concrete core and wings step in as the tower rises, which helps reduce wind forces. A steel spire tops the building."),
];
static SCIENCE_STRUCTURES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["longest bridge", "longest bridges", "longest bridge in the world"], "The longest bridge in the world is the Danyang-Kunshan Grand Bridge on the Beijing-Shanghai high-speed railway at about 164 km. The Hong Kong-Zhuhai-Macau crossing is 55 km including its tunnel, with 48.3 km of bridge over water. A long bridge is nearly always a run of short spans on piers rather than one big span."),
    (&["suspension bridge", "suspension bridges"], "A suspension bridge hangs its deck from two main cables slung over the towers and anchored into the ground at each end, with vertical hangers dropping to the deck. The cables pull, the towers carry that pull down, and the anchors take it at the ends, which is why it can span 1 to 2 km - the Humber Bridge, the Golden Gate, the Akashi Kaikyo."),
    (&["cable stayed", "cable-stayed", "cable stayed bridge"], "A cable-stayed bridge runs cables straight from the tower to the deck, a fan or a harp of them, instead of one main cable with hangers. It needs no big anchorage at the ends, so it suits spans of a few hundred metres - the Queen Elizabeth II Bridge at Dartford and the Millau Viaduct are cable-stayed."),
    (&["box girder", "box girders", "box girder bridge"], "A box girder is a beam made as a hollow box of steel or concrete, wide at the top to carry the road and deep enough to be stiff: a hollow box is far stiffer than a solid bar of the same weight, because the material sits out at the edges. Most long concrete bridges and cable-stayed decks are a box girder, and the box is also where the cables and the air for the inside run."),
    (&["pier", "piers"], "A pier is one of the upright supports a bridge span rests on, and it is what the weight of the span comes down through to the ground. In a river they are built inside a cofferdam or a caisson, and the spans sit on bearings on top of them so the deck can expand and contract without cracking."),
    (&["beam bridge", "beam bridges", "girder bridge", "girder bridges"], "A beam bridge is the simplest kind there is: a slab or a girder laid across two supports. The deck bends under the load, so the top is squeezed and the bottom is stretched, and the deeper the girder the further it reaches. Cheap, quick, and right for a short span."),
    (&["arch bridge", "arch bridges", "how does an arch work", "how do arches work", "why do arches work"], "An arch bridge carries its load in compression: the weight comes down through the arch and pushes outwards at the bottom, so the ground or the abutments at each end have to resist that push. That is why stone and brick are so good in an arch - they take squeezing and not stretching - and why the Iron Bridge at Coalbrookdale, the first big cast-iron arch, was built in 1779 in the same shape."),
    (&["viaduct", "viaducts", "aqueduct", "aqueducts"], "A viaduct carries a road or a railway across a valley on a run of arches or piers rather than one span, which is what you need when the gap is long and the ground below is uneven - the Ribblehead Viaduct has 24 arches. An aqueduct is the same structure carrying water: the Romans built them on stone arches, and a canal aqueduct does the same job for boats."),
    (&["cantilever", "cantilevers", "cantilever bridge", "cantilever bridges"], "A cantilever is a beam held at one end with the other end free: a balcony, a diving board, a crane jib. Two cantilevers reaching out from opposite banks make a cantilever bridge, and it can be built outwards from each side without scaffolding in the water - the Forth Bridge is the famous one."),
    (&["expansion joint", "expansion joints"], "Bridges have expansion joints because steel and concrete change length as they warm and cool, and a long deck moves by several centimetres between winter and summer. The joint is the strip that lets it happen without cracking the deck or shoving the abutments - the toothed metal you hear clicking under the wheels - and it is also what lets the bridge move in an earthquake."),
    (&["tunnel", "tunnels"], "One way to build a tunnel under water is to sink prefabricated sections into a trench, join them and cover them over. The Oresund link uses this method, as does the Fehmarnbelt tunnel under construction. Another way is to bore through the rock below the seabed, as engineers did through the chalk under the Channel."),
];
static RECYCLING_ITEM_ROUTES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["tin", "tins", "tin can", "tin cans", "cans", "drink can", "drink cans", "food tin", "food tins", "baked bean tin", "soup tin", "pet food tin", "aluminium"], "Empty and rinse food tins and drink cans, then put them in your metal recycling as your council directs. Check the council's instructions for partly full aerosols, and keep them away from heat. Do not puncture them."),
    (&["foil", "tin foil", "kitchen foil", "foil tray", "foil trays", "foil container", "bacofoil"], "Clean aluminium foil and trays can go in household recycling in England. Remove food and follow your council's instructions on how to put the foil out. Foil-lined plastic pouches and greasy foil need a different route."),
    (&["jar", "jars", "glass jar", "glass jars", "glass bottle", "glass bottles", "wine bottle", "beer bottle", "jam jar", "sauce jar"], "Put empty, rinsed glass bottles and jars in your household glass recycling. Your council explains how to handle the lids. Drinking glasses and heat-resistant cookware need a different route from bottles and jars."),
    (&["paper", "newspaper", "newspapers", "card", "cardboard", "carton", "cartons", "junk mail", "envelope", "envelopes", "magazine", "magazines", "cereal box", "cereal boxes"], "Put clean, dry paper and flattened cardboard in household recycling. Check how your council wants drink cartons put out. Remove greasy or food-soiled sections from cardboard before recycling the clean parts."),
    (&["plastic bottle", "plastic bottles", "bottle", "bottles", "tub", "tubs", "yoghurt pot", "yoghurt pots", "margarine tub", "plastic tub", "plastic tubs", "milk bottle", "shampoo bottle"], "Empty and rinse plastic bottles and tubs for household recycling. Follow the council's instructions on lids and trays, including black plastic trays."),
    (&["crisp packet", "crisp packets", "carrier bag", "carrier bags", "plastic bag", "plastic bags", "bread bag", "film lid", "cling film", "bubble wrap", "soft plastic", "padded envelope"], "Check your council's list for film and bags, such as crisp packets and bread bags. If the local collection does not accept them, some supermarkets take certain clean, dry soft plastics. Check the collection bin's list before taking them."),
    (&["polystyrene", "styrofoam", "packing peanuts", "meat tray", "meat trays", "takeaway box", "takeaway boxes"], "Expanded polystyrene generally goes in residual household waste. Check your council's list for trays, since some look similar but are made from a plastic that can be recycled."),
    (&["nappy", "nappies", "diaper", "diapers", "baby wipes", "wet wipes", "sanitary", "sanitary pad", "sanitary pads", "tampon", "tampons", "incontinence"], "Nappies, wipes, pads and tampons all go in the general waste - nothing absorbent is recycled, and a wet wipe is not flushable however the packet reads, because it does not break down and it is most of what blocks a sewer. Bag it and bin it; a nappy bin with a lid keeps the room livable."),
    (&["clothes", "clothing", "old clothes", "textiles", "textile", "shoes", "old shoes", "curtains", "bedding", "duvet", "duvets", "towels", "rags"], "Take clean, dry reusable clothes and shoes to a charity shop or clothing bank that accepts them. Check your council's textile list for worn items, bedding, duvets and pillows. The routes vary by area."),
];
static CLEANING_SOFT_STAINS: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["ink", "inks", "inked", "biro", "ballpoint", "ball point", "pen", "pens", "marker", "markers", "sharpie", "felt tip", "felt-tip", "rollerball", "highlighter"], "Blot the ink from the outside in. Check the upholstery label and use a compatible spot cleaner, tested in a hidden spot first. Some carpet makers allow rubbing alcohol for ballpoint ink. Check the manufacturer's instructions for yours and keep solvents away from heat."),
    (&["grease", "greasy", "greased", "oil", "oily", "oiled", "fat", "fatty", "butter", "margarine", "mayonnaise", "mayo", "lard", "dripping", "ghee", "olive oil", "cooking oil", "chip fat"], "Lift any solid grease with a spoon. If the label allows water-based cleaning, blot with a cloth dampened with a little diluted mild detergent. Test on a hidden spot first, then blot with clean water to remove residue."),
    (&["ketchup", "tomato sauce", "tomato ketchup", "curry", "curry sauce", "gravy", "bolognese", "bolognaise", "chilli", "chili", "chilli sauce", "chili sauce", "salsa", "soy sauce", "sriracha", "hot sauce", "pasta sauce", "stew", "casserole", "jam", "marmalade", "honey", "syrup", "golden syrup", "ice cream", "yoghurt", "yogurt", "custard", "soup", "smoothie", "milkshake", "egg"], "Scoop up what is still sitting there with a spoon, then dab, don't rub, with cool water and a drop of washing-up liquid from the outside of the mark in, blotting with a towel as the colour lifts. Rinse by dabbing with clean water and blot dry, and keep heat away from it: a hairdryer or an iron sets a sauce stain for good."),
    (&["chocolate", "choc", "nutella", "chocolate spread", "chocolate mousse", "hot chocolate", "cocoa"], "Let the chocolate firm up and lift what you can with a blunt spoon. If the surface can be cleaned with water, dab with a cloth dampened with diluted mild detergent, then blot with clean water and dry with a towel."),
    (&["blood", "bloody", "bleeding", "nosebleed", "period", "cut myself"], "Wear gloves when handling someone else's blood. On fabric that can be cleaned with water, blot from the outside in with cool water and a suitable cleaner. Follow the care label, rinse by blotting, then blot dry."),
    (&["mud", "muddy", "soil", "clay", "grit", "sand"], "Let it dry completely, then vacuum off what breaks loose - a wet mud patch only gets pushed into the backing. Dab at what is left with cool water and a drop of washing-up liquid, then blot dry with a towel, working from the outside in."),
    (&["candle", "candles", "wax", "candle wax", "wax crayon", "crayon", "crayons"], "Let the wax harden and lift off what you can with a blunt plastic edge. Ask the carpet or upholstery maker about the residue before using heat or a solvent, since either can damage the material."),
];
static BUILD_JOBS: &[(&[&str], &str, &str, &str)] = &[
    (&["bike shed", "bike store", "bike storage"], "An hour or two for two of you, and the base is the whole job.", "A drill with masonry bits, a spanner, a spirit level and a tape measure; the fixings to the wall come with it and the ground anchor does not.", "Follow the kit instructions for a level base and fixings. Check local planning rules before putting up a permanent structure."),
    (&["summerhouse", "summer house", "log cabin"], "Two of you: two days for a cabin-style one, and the base and the roof are a day each.", "A drill and driver, a spirit level, a stepladder, clamps, a saw and a mallet; the windows and the roofing felt come with it.", "Follow the kit instructions for a level base and fixings. Check local planning rules before putting up a permanent structure."),
    (&["gazebo", "gazebos", "pop up gazebo"], "Twenty minutes for two of you with a pop-up one; a wooden one is a day.", "Nothing for a pop-up beyond its own frame and pegs; a wooden one wants a drill, a spirit level and a mallet.", "Peg every leg and add the guy ropes, even on a still day, because a pop-up gazebo is a kite with a roof."),
    (&["climbing frame", "climbing frames", "swing set", "swingset", "wooden swing"], "A day for two of you, and digging the leg holes is half of it.", "A post hole digger, a spirit level, a drill and driver, a tape measure, a saw and a socket set.", "Anchor it exactly as the manufacturer's instructions specify. Check the frame for stability, enough clearance around it and a suitable surface before anyone uses it."),
    (&["raised bed", "raised beds"], "An afternoon for two of you, and filling it takes longer than building it.", "A saw, a drill and driver, a spirit level, a tape measure and a staple gun for the liner.", "Line the inside with a membrane if the timber is treated, and leave the boards with a gap at the bottom for water."),
    (&["shed", "sheds"], "Two of you: a day for a shed up to 8x6, and the base is a day of its own.", "A drill with wood and masonry bits, a tape measure, a spirit level, a stepladder, a mallet, a knife for the felt and a screwdriver; the screws come with it and the base does not.", "Follow the kit instructions for a level base and fixings. Check local planning rules before putting up a permanent structure."),
    (&["playhouse", "play house", "wendy house", "cubby house"], "Two of you: two to three hours, and a plastic one that clicks together is less.", "A screwdriver, a mallet, a spirit level and a tape measure; a wooden one comes with its screws but rarely with a base.", "Stand it on something flat, a paved square or a timber frame, or the door will not line up and the walls will not meet at the corners."),
    (&["greenhouse", "green house"], "Two of you: a weekend for an aluminium 6x8, and a day of that is getting the base right.", "A drill with masonry bits, a spirit level, a stepladder, a tape measure, clamps and silicone; the glazing clips and the glass come with the frame.", "The base has to be square and level to within a few millimetres, because the glass is cut to fit and will not go in otherwise."),
    (&["fence panel", "fence panels", "feather edge", "closeboard"], "About an hour a panel for two of you once the posts are in, and the posts want a day to set first.", "A post hole spade, a spirit level, a drill and driver, a saw and a string line; a panel is light but it is a sail, so this is a two-person job in any wind.", "Fix the panels to the posts, not the other way round, and brace the end posts until the concrete has gone off."),
    (&["decking", "deck boards", "deck board", "raised deck"], "A 3x3 metre deck is two or three weekends for one person, and the frame is most of it.", "A saw, a drill and driver, a string line, a spirit level, a set square, clamps and stainless screws.", "Level the frame and set the joists at 400mm centres before a board goes down, and leave a gap of about 5mm between boards so the water runs off."),
    (&["wardrobe", "wardrobes", "flat pack", "flat-pack", "flatpack", "chest of drawers", "bed frame", "bookcase", "sideboard"], "Two of you: two to four hours, and the doors and drawers are half of it.", "A Pozidriv screwdriver, a mallet, a tape measure and the Allen key that comes in the box; a cordless driver on its lowest clutch saves your wrists.", "Build the unit as the instructions show and fit the recommended anti-tip hardware to a suitable wall. Check for pipes and cables before drilling."),
    (&["kitchen unit", "kitchen units", "base units", "wall units", "kitchen cabinets", "fit a kitchen", "fitting a kitchen", "kitchen fitting", "kitchen install", "installing a kitchen"], "A run of five units is a day if you have done it before and two or three the first time, and the worktop is a day of its own.", "A spirit level, clamps, a drill and driver, a jigsaw, a tape measure and packers.", "Set the first unit dead level and square to the wall and clamp the next to it: everything after that one follows it, so ten minutes spent there saves the whole run."),
    (&["trampoline", "trampolines"], "Two of you: an hour or two, and the springs are most of it.", "Two spanners, which are usually in the box, gloves, and the spring puller that comes with it.", "Follow the manual for ground clearance, anchoring and assembly. Fit the enclosure and pads and check everything is secure before anyone uses it."),
];
static WATER_OUT_REPLIES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["bath", "baths", "bathtub", "bath tub"], "Open the bath plug first. If the drain is blocked, scoop water out into a bucket and carry it to a drain that can safely take it. Avoid lifting a full bucket if it is too heavy. Avoid mixing drain cleaners, and call a plumber if the blockage persists."),
    (&["caravan", "caravans", "motorhome", "motor home", "camper van", "campervan", "camper"], "Check the caravan and water-heater manuals for winter draining. Switch off the water heater and pump as directed and let the water cool before opening the specified drain valve. Catch and dispose of the waste water at a suitable point."),
    (&["water feature", "water fountain", "fountain", "fountains", "waterfall", "water spout", "water wall"], "Switch off and disconnect the pump from a dry, safe point before touching the water. Use the feature's drain plug or the method in the manual. Avoid running the pump dry."),
    (&["bilge", "bilges", "boat bilge"], "If a boat is taking on water and people are in danger, alert the Coastguard immediately, put on lifejackets and follow their instructions. Use the bilge pump if it is safe to do so. If you are draining a moored boat for maintenance, follow the manual and check for leaks before going out."),
    (&["hose", "hoses", "hose pipe", "hosepipe", "garden hose", "watering hose"], "Take the nozzle off and walk the hose out straight downhill, lifting it hand over hand so the water runs to the low end - a minute's job and it does not kink. Coil it loosely and hang it with both ends down so what is left drips out, and bring it into the shed or indoors before the first frost: a hose left holding water splits when it freezes, and one put away wet grows slime inside."),
    (&["freezer", "freezers", "chest freezer", "fridge freezer", "ice box"], "Follow the freezer's defrost instructions. Disconnect the power and move food to another freezer or an insulated cooler. Use the drain provided or catch melting water in a shallow tray. Let ice melt without chipping it with a sharp tool."),
    (&["waterbed", "water bed", "water beds"], "Follow the waterbed maker's instructions for the pump and any heater, and use a suitable drain. Drain the mattress completely before moving it. Keep water away from electrical fittings."),
    (&["fish tank", "fish tanks", "aquarium", "fish bowl"], "For routine cleaning, siphon only part of the aquarium water. Use replacement water treated to remove chlorine and suitable for the species and temperature. Keep the filter media wet in aquarium water. If the entire tank has to move or be emptied, arrange a suitable temporary tank first."),
    (&["water tank", "header tank", "cold water tank", "loft tank", "cold tank"], "Follow the manufacturer's instructions or use a plumber to isolate and drain the tank. Header tanks, drinking-water tanks and sealed heating systems are different. Use the specified drain route and keep water away from electrics. Do not alter a sealed or hot-water system unless qualified."),
];
static KIT_SUBSTITUTES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["colander", "colanders", "strainer", "strainers"], "A heatproof sieve over the sink can replace a colander for a small amount. For a larger pan, use a slotted spoon to lift the food out and leave the water to cool before emptying it."),
    (&["sieve", "sieves", "sifter", "sifters"], "A fine colander or a tea strainer does it, and if you have neither, whisk the flour round a wide bowl for half a minute: the whisk breaks the lumps and lets the air in the same way sifting does."),
    (&["whisk", "whisks", "balloon whisk", "hand whisk", "electric whisk"], "A fork beats eggs and cream, just slower: keep the bowl deep and the wrist loose, and for cream chill both bowl and cream first. A clean jam jar with the lid on will whip a small amount of cream in about a minute of shaking."),
    (&["rolling pin", "rolling pins"], "Press and stretch the dough with clean hands, or use a smooth, sturdy food-safe cylinder dusted with flour. Avoid glass that could crack when pressed."),
    (&["grater", "graters", "box grater", "cheese grater"], "A vegetable peeler makes shavings of hard cheese and chocolate, and a knife does for anything going into a sauce. For parmesan or zest, a fine Microplane is the tool worth buying; until then, chop it as small as you can and it melts the same."),
    (&["blender", "blenders", "liquidiser", "liquidizer"], "A stick blender in a deep jug does most of it, and a food processor does the rest. A masher then a sieve gets you a smooth soup without either, and a jar with a tight lid shaken hard will dress a small amount of sauce."),
    (&["food processor", "food processors", "processor", "magimix"], "A stick blender for anything wet, a box grater for anything hard, and a knife for anything rough. Chopping an onion small by hand takes two minutes; a blender turns it to water, so keep the blade out of it."),
    (&["measuring jug", "measuring jugs", "jug", "measuring cup"], "A mug is about 250 to 300 ml, and a tablespoon is 15 ml and a teaspoon 5. Anything you would weigh, weigh in grams instead: for water and milk a millilitre is a gram, so 200 ml is 200g on the scales."),
    (&["tin opener", "tin openers", "can opener", "can openers"], "Use a ring-pull tin or borrow a can opener. A knife or spoon can slip or leave sharp edges and small pieces of metal in the food."),
    (&["corkscrew", "corkscrews", "bottle opener", "bottle openers"], "Borrow a corkscrew for a cork or a bottle opener for a crown cap. If neither is available, leave the bottle sealed. Avoid striking, heating or cutting the bottle."),
    (&["potato masher", "mashers", "potato mashers"], "A fork for anything soft, and the end of a wooden spoon for potatoes. A ricer or a sieve and the back of a spoon gives a smoother mash than a masher ever does, which is what you want for gnocchi."),
    (&["cake tin", "cake tins", "round tin", "springform tin", "tins"], "A roasting tin lined with paper works: bake it as a traybake at the same temperature, checking five minutes earlier, and cut it into squares. A deep frying pan with a lid will bake a shallow cake on the hob if the lid goes on and the heat stays low."),
    (&["loaf tin", "loaf tins", "bread tin"], "A roasting tin or a deep ovenproof saucepan lined with baking paper. Free-standing bread bakes perfectly well on a lined tray; the tin is there for the shape, and a round loaf tastes the same."),
    (&["baking parchment", "parchment", "greaseproof paper", "baking paper", "silicon paper"], "Grease the tin well with butter and dust it with flour, or use a silicone mat, or foil brushed with oil. Parchment and greaseproof paper are not the same thing: greaseproof sticks to a cake, so grease it again on top if that is all you have."),
    (&["cling film", "clingfilm", "plastic wrap", "saran wrap"], "A plate over the bowl, or a clean damp tea towel, keeps a surface from drying. For dough left to rise, an upturned bowl over the whole thing is better than film anyway."),
    (&["muslin", "muslins", "cheesecloth", "jelly bag"], "A clean tea towel, a new J-cloth or a pair of clean tights works for jelly and for straining yoghurt. Scalding the cloth first with boiling water keeps it from tasting of soap, and hang it over a bowl with the corners tied to a cupboard handle."),
    (&["piping bag", "piping bags", "icing bag"], "A freezer bag with a corner snipped off, or a sheet of baking paper rolled into a cone and taped. A good zip-lock bag will take a star nozzle if you cut the corner to its width."),
    (&["skewers", "skewer", "kebab sticks", "bamboo skewers"], "Rosemary stems stripped of their leaves make fine skewers for lamb and chicken, and metal kebab sticks or a cake tester do the testing job. To test a cake, a knife blade or a strand of dry spaghetti works as well as a skewer."),
    (&["thermometer", "thermometers", "meat thermometer", "sugar thermometer"], "A clean food probe is the best way to check meat. If you cannot measure the temperature of poultry or pork, check the thickest part is steaming hot throughout, with clear juices and no pink meat. For a sugar recipe that needs a precise temperature, use a sugar thermometer and follow the recipe."),
    (&["funnel", "funnels"], "For dry ingredients, fold clean stiff paper into a cone. For liquids, especially hot liquids, use a heat-safe jug with a spout. Pour slowly into a stable container."),
    (&["mortar and pestle", "pestle and mortar", "mortar", "pestle"], "A bowl and the end of a rolling pin crushes spices, or put them in a bag and roll over it with a tin. Whole spices toasted in a dry pan first crush far more easily, whatever you crush them with."),
    (&["slotted spoon", "slotted spoons"], "A fork, or a normal spoon held back against the side of the pan as you lift, drains the same. A fish slice with holes does the job for anything bigger than a pea."),
    (&["tongs", "tongs", "tongs"], "Two forks back to back, or a spatula and a spoon. Chopsticks are the best stand-in there is for turning small things in a hot pan."),
    (&["pastry brush", "pastry brushes", "basting brush"], "Your fingertips for milk or egg wash, the back of a spoon for oil, and a scrunched piece of kitchen paper for glazing a pie. None of them shed bristles, which is the one thing the brush is for."),
    (&["fridge", "fridges", "refrigerator", "refrigerators"], "Choose shelf-stable food until you have reliable chilling. Keep food that needs refrigeration at 5°C or below using a fridge or cooler whose temperature you can check. Follow the storage label. Avoid using a windowsill or garage for perishable food unless you can maintain the right temperature."),
    (&["freezer", "freezers", "freezer compartment"], "Buy and cook smaller amounts if you have no freezer. Keep cooked leftovers in a working fridge and use them within 48 hours, or follow the shorter limit for the food. Choose shelf-stable food if you cannot refrigerate it."),
    (&["kettle", "kettles", "electric kettle"], "Boil water in a saucepan on the hob and pour carefully with a heatproof jug if needed. Use a vessel and method the appliance instructions allow if you want to heat water in a microwave."),
    (&["hob", "hobs", "stove", "stovetop"], "If the hob is unavailable, choose a recipe designed for the oven or microwave you have and use a suitable dish. Check pack instructions for frozen or ready-made foods, and keep any camping stove outdoors."),
    (&["plunger", "plungers", "sink plunger", "toilet plunger"], "For a minor sink blockage, remove and clean a removable strainer if you can do it safely. Use a proper plunger if you have one and avoid mixing drain cleaners. Call a plumber if the drain backs up or stays blocked."),
    (&["mop", "mops", "floor mop"], "A cloth and a bucket: work backwards to the door, wringing as you go, and a dry towel under your feet dries the last of it. On a small floor a sponge and a bowl is quicker than a mop anyway."),
    (&["hoover", "hoovers", "vacuum", "vacuum cleaner", "vacuums"], "A broom or a stiff brush for a hard floor, and a damp rubber glove or a damp cloth for hair on carpet - it gathers in one swipe. A lint roller lifts crumbs and pet hair off a sofa or a stair carpet."),
    (&["stepladder", "stepladders", "step ladder", "ladder", "ladders"], "Borrow or hire a suitable stepladder and use it on firm, level ground. For jobs you cannot reach safely, wait until you have the right equipment or ask a professional."),
    (&["drill", "drills", "power drill", "cordless drill"], "Use an adhesive hook rated for the load only where the surface and fitting are suitable. For anything that needs drilling, check for cables and pipes and use the right detector, fixings and tools. Tapping a wall cannot tell you what is behind it."),
    (&["hammer", "hammers", "mallet", "mallets"], "Borrow or hire a suitable hammer or mallet. Use the correct tool for the fastener and surface so it does not slip or shatter."),
    (&["spirit level", "level", "levels", "bubble level"], "A phone has a level in it, and a clear bottle half full of water on its side reads the same way: the bubble sits between the two marks. For a shelf, measure down from the ceiling at both ends - a shelf that matches the ceiling looks level even when it isn't."),
    (&["stud finder", "stud finders", "stud detector"], "Use an adhesive hook rated for the load only where the surface and fitting are suitable. For anything that needs drilling, check for cables and pipes and use the right detector, fixings and tools. Tapping a wall cannot tell you what is behind it."),
    (&["garden strimmer", "strimmer", "strimmers", "weed whacker", "weed trimmer"], "Shears, or a pair of long-handled edging shears, cut the same grass at the edges and cost nothing to run. Cut little and often rather than letting it get long - long grass wraps round any machine, including the borrowed one."),
];
const CHILLI_SHORTAGE: &str = "\\b(?:but no|and no|no|without|short of|missing|havent got|haven't got|not got|ran out of|run out of)\\s+((?:[a-z']+\\s*){0,3})";
const CHILLI_BEAN_STEMS: &[&str] = &["bean", "kidney", "pinto", "cannelli", "haricot", "baked", "black"];
const COOKING_FAT_CARD: &str = "Oil takes the heat and butter gives the taste: butter starts to burn at about 150C, so for a hot pan or a hot oven use oil and add butter at the end. Butter on its own is for a low pan where nothing goes above that heat - onions, mushrooms, eggs, 15 to 20 minutes on a low heat for the onions - and in a cake oil keeps it moist for days while butter gives the flavour most people expect.";
const COOKING_FAT_DECIDER: &[&str] = &["For onions either is fine: butter on a low heat for 15 to 20 minutes gives the taste and will not burn below 150C, and oil takes a hotter pan - so oil for a hard fry and butter for the onions.", "Low heat for butter, high heat for oil: onions in butter on a low heat for 15 to 20 minutes sweeten without catching, and a pan hot enough to brown goes to the oil."];
const DIFFERENCES: &[(&[&str], &[&str], &str)] = &[
    (&["jam", "jams"], &["marmalade", "marmalades"], "Jam is fruit cooked with sugar until it sets, and it is soft enough to spread; marmalade is citrus - usually Seville oranges - cooked with sugar and shreds of peel, so it is firmer and has a bitter edge. Same method, different fruit: anything with peel shreds in it is marmalade."),
    (&["lake", "lakes"], &["pond", "ponds"], "Ponds are small and shallow: light reaches the bottom across most of one, so plants grow right through the middle. A lake is deep enough to have open water in the middle, with its own wind-driven ripples and a shore where plants stop. There is no exact size that splits them, but a pond you could wade across is a pond."),
    (&["wage", "wages", "hourly pay"], &["salary", "salaries", "yearly pay"], "A wage is paid for the hours you worked, so overtime adds and a short week takes away; a salary is one yearly figure paid in twelve equal months whatever the hours. Hourly pay is better when you work extra, a salary is steadier when work is quiet."),
    (&["tortoise", "tortoises"], &["turtle", "turtles", "terrapin", "terrapins"], "Tortoises live on land: domed shell, stumpy elephant-like back legs, and they cannot swim. Turtles live in water: a flatter, streamlined shell and flippers, and the sea turtles only come ashore to lay. A terrapin is the small freshwater turtle people keep in a pond."),
    (&["frog", "frogs"], &["toad", "toads"], "A frog has smooth, damp skin, long back legs and jumps; a toad has dry, warty skin, shorter legs and walks or makes small hops. Frogs stay near water and toads wander further from it, coming back only to breed, and toads lay their eggs in strings rather than clumps."),
    (&["baking powder"], &["bicarbonate of soda", "bicarb", "bicarbonate", "baking soda"], "Bicarbonate of soda is an alkali on its own: it only fizzes when it meets an acid such as buttermilk, lemon or vinegar. Baking powder is bicarbonate with that acid and a little starch already mixed in, so it raises on its own - which is why a recipe can call for three times as much baking powder as it would bicarb, and why swapping one for the other flatly does not work."),
    (&["stock", "stocks"], &["broth", "broths"], "Stock is bones and scraps simmered for hours, and it sets to a jelly when cold because of the gelatine; it is an ingredient, not a dish. Broth is usually simmered for an hour or two with meat and vegetables still in it and is eaten as it is. Same pot, different job."),
    (&["stew", "stews"], &["casserole", "casseroles"], "A stew is meat and vegetables cooked slowly in liquid on top of the hob; a casserole is the same food cooked in a covered dish in the oven. The dish gives the name, not the recipe, so a casserole is a stew in a lidded pot."),
    (&["muffin", "muffins"], &["cupcake", "cupcakes"], "A cupcake is a small cake - butter, sugar, eggs, flour - baked in a paper case and iced. In Britain a muffin is a flat, round yeast bread, split and toasted; in America a muffin is a soft, barely sweet quick bread with no icing at all. So a muffin is bread and a cupcake is cake."),
    (&["scone", "scones"], &["rock cake", "rock cakes", "rock bun", "rock buns"], "A scone is a soft dough rolled and cut, baked hot for 10 to 12 minutes, and it should be light enough to break open. A rock cake is a stiff dough with dried fruit dropped in rough lumps, so it bakes into a craggy top - heavier, sweeter, and it keeps for days where a scone does not."),
    (&["solicitor", "solicitors"], &["barrister", "barristers"], "A solicitor does the day-to-day work: contracts, letters, a house purchase, the first meeting. A barrister is the one who argues in court, and you reach one through a solicitor. A lawyer is the general word for both."),
    (&["hire", "hiring"], &["rent", "renting"], "You hire a thing for hours or days - a van, a carpet cleaner, a hall - and rent a home or a piece of land for months or years. Hire is short and usually about the item, rent is long and usually about the place."),
    (&["bank", "banks", "high street bank"], &["building society", "building societies", "mutual"], "A bank is owned by shareholders; a building society is owned by the people who save with it and borrow from it, which is why its profit goes back out as better rates rather than a dividend. Your money is protected the same either way - £85,000 per person per bank or society under the FSCS - and the card, the ISA and the app work the same. Choose on the rate, not the label."),
    (&["sale", "sales", "in the sale"], &["offer", "offers", "on offer"], "An offer is a lower price on one thing, usually for a set time; a sale is a shop cutting prices across the board. Neither word has a legal meaning, so the price on the till is the one that counts - and your right to a refund on something in a sale is exactly the same as outside one."),
    (&["envy", "envious", "jealousy of"], &["jealous", "jealousy"], "Envy is wanting what someone else has: their house, their job, their holiday. Jealousy is the fear of losing what you have, and it needs a third person - a partner's new friend, a colleague who is close to your boss. So envy looks outwards and jealousy guards something."),
    (&["warranty", "warranties", "extended warranty"], &["guarantee", "guarantees", "manufacturer guarantee"], "A guarantee is the maker's own promise to repair or replace the thing within a set time, usually a year or two and often only if you registered it. A warranty is usually the extra cover a shop sells you. Neither replaces your rights against the retailer: in England and Wales a faulty product can be put right for up to six years, warranty or no warranty."),
    (&["sprain", "sprains", "sprained"], &["strain", "strains", "pulled muscle", "pulled a muscle"], "A sprain is a stretched or torn ligament - the band joining bone to bone - so it happens at a joint, most often a twisted ankle. A strain is a stretched or torn muscle or tendon in the meat of a leg, back or arm. The first day is the same for both: rest it, ice for 20 minutes every few hours, wrap it, keep it up; see someone if you cannot put weight on it."),
    (&["fracture", "fractures", "fractured", "broken bone", "broken bones", "hairline fracture"], &["break", "breaks", "broken", "broke"], "Nothing: a fracture and a break are the same injury, and a doctor saying fracture is not saying it is worse. A hairline or stress fracture is a fine crack, and a greenstick fracture is a bone bent and cracked on one side, which is what children get. What changes the treatment is not the word but where the bone is and whether the ends still line up, so ask about that instead."),
    (&["leasehold", "leaseholder", "leaseholders"], &["freehold", "freeholder", "freeholders"], "Freehold means you own the building and the land under it; leasehold means you own the right to live there for a fixed number of years and someone else owns the building and the ground. A leaseholder pays ground rent and usually a service charge, and the lease gets shorter every year, which is what a buyer and a mortgage lender look at. Most flats are leasehold and most houses freehold."),
    (&["flat", "flats"], &["apartment", "apartments"], "Nothing: a flat and an apartment are the same home, and the choice is taste - flat is the everyday British word, apartment the estate agent's and the usual one in America and for a serviced block. A maisonette is the one that differs, being on two floors with its own entrance."),
    (&["annual", "annuals", "annual plant"], &["perennial", "perennials", "perennial plant"], "An annual grows, flowers and dies within one year, so it is sown again every spring; a perennial comes back from the same roots year after year. Annuals flower for months and fill a bed cheaply, perennials cost more and give a few weeks each year, so most beds use both."),
    (&["mulch", "mulching", "mulched"], &["compost", "composting", "composted"], "Compost is rotted organic matter dug into the soil to feed it; mulch is anything spread on top of the soil to hold water and keep weeds down, and it can be compost, bark, manure or gravel. So compost improves the soil and mulch is a blanket: you can mulch with compost, but you do not dig gravel in."),
    (&["boil", "boils", "a boil on"], &["cyst", "cysts"], "A boil is a deep infection of a hair follicle: tender, hot, and it swells up and comes to a head. A cyst is a closed sac under the skin, usually smooth and painless, holding fluid or keratin, and it does not drain and heal by itself. A boil often needs an antibiotic and to drain; a cyst that keeps filling is cut out. Do not squeeze either."),
    (&["lager", "lagers"], &["ale", "ales", "bitter", "real ale"], "Lager and ale are both beer, brewed with different yeast: lager uses a yeast that works cold at the bottom of the tank, ale a yeast that works warm at the top. Cold and slow gives lager its clean, crisp taste; warm and quick gives ale its fruitiness and its hops. Stout and porter are ales brewed dark, so colour says nothing about which one you have."),
    (&["lager", "lagers", "ale", "ales", "stout", "stouts", "porter", "pilsner"], &["beer", "beers"], "Beer is the whole family, and lager and ale are the two branches of it: lager is brewed cold with a bottom yeast, ale warm with a top one. So every lager is a beer and not every beer is a lager - bitter, stout, porter and wheat beer are all ales. If a pub list says \"beers\" it is offering both."),
    (&["rabbit", "rabbits", "bunny", "bunnies"], &["hare", "hares"], "A hare is much bigger and longer-legged than a rabbit, with long ears tipped black, and it lives above ground in a form - a shallow scrape - rather than a burrow. Hares are born fully furred with their eyes open and are running within hours; rabbits are born blind and bald in a lined nest underground. Hares are also solitary and rabbits live in groups, so a big, loping, lonely one in a field is a hare."),
    (&["moped", "mopeds", "scooter", "scooters"], &["motorbike", "motorbikes", "motorcycle", "motorcycles"], "A moped is up to 50cc and 28mph, and you can ride one at 16 on a provisional licence after a CBT - that is what the pedals-then-engine history of the name means. A motorbike is anything bigger, from a 125 at 17 to a full licence, and it needs the tests, insurance and a proper helmet. A scooter is a shape rather than a class: most small ones are moped-legal at 50cc."),
    (&["heart attack", "heart attacks"], &["panic attack", "panic attacks", "anxiety attack"], "A heart attack is a blocked artery: heavy, crushing pressure in the middle of the chest, often spreading to the left arm, the jaw or the back, with sweating, sickness and breathlessness, brought on by effort and lasting more than fifteen minutes. A panic attack peaks within ten minutes: racing heart, tingling hands, dizziness and a fear of dying, usually at rest, and it eases as you breathe slowly. Chest pain that is new, crushing or comes with sweating is a 999 call - do not wait to work out which one it is."),
    (&["heart rate", "pulse", "pulse rate", "bpm", "beats per minute"], &["blood pressure", "bp", "readings", "reading"], "Heart rate is how fast the heart beats, counted in beats a minute: 60 to 100 at rest is normal, and it rises with exercise, caffeine and stress. Blood pressure is how hard the blood pushes on the artery walls, written as two numbers - the top one when the heart squeezes, the bottom one between beats - and around 120/80 is a good reading. A fast pulse and a high pressure are separate things: you can have one without the other."),
    (&["pill", "pills", "tablet", "tablets"], &["capsule", "capsules"], "A tablet is powder pressed into a disc, and it can be split or crushed (unless the packet says otherwise, because some are coated to release slowly). A capsule is a shell - usually gelatine or a vegetable gum - filled with powder, granules or liquid, so it is swallowed whole and opens in the stomach. If swallowing is the problem, ask the pharmacist before crushing: many tablets are not meant to be."),
    (&["magpie", "magpies"], &["crow", "crows", "carrion crow", "rook", "rooks"], "A magpie is black and white with a long tail, about 45cm, and it is unmistakable. A crow is all black, bigger than a pigeon and often alone or in a pair, with a heavy bill and a harsh caw; rooks are the ones in noisy flocks with bare grey faces. If it has any white on it, it is not a crow."),
    (&["maisonette", "maisonettes"], &["flat", "flats", "apartment", "apartments", "bungalow"], "A maisonette is a flat on two floors with its own front door to the outside, so it has stairs inside and no shared hall. A flat is usually on one level off a shared entrance, and a bungalow is a house with everything on the ground floor. A maisonette feels more like a house and is often leasehold all the same, so check the lease and the service charge."),
    (&["mortgage broker", "brokers", "broker", "independent adviser"], &["bank", "banks", "building society", "lender", "lenders"], "A broker does not lend the money: they compare the deals on the market and apply to a lender for you, and many charge a fee on top of the lender's arrangement fee. Going to a bank directly means one lender's own deals with no broker fee, which is fine if your case is straightforward. A whole-of-market broker earns its fee when your pay is unusual, your deposit is small or your credit file is untidy."),
    (&["hp", "hire purchase", "hp agreement", "hp finance"], &["pcp", "personal contract purchase", "pcp finance", "pcp deal"], "With hire purchase you pay a deposit and fixed monthly payments, and at the end of the term the car is yours - nothing more to pay. With PCP you pay a smaller deposit and smaller months for a mileage limit, then choose at the end: hand the car back, part-exchange it, or pay a large final payment to keep it. PCP is cheaper per month and costs more over the years, and excess miles are charged at a few pence each, so HP suits keeping a car and PCP suits changing it every three years."),
    (&["ukulele", "uke", "ukuleles"], &["guitar", "guitars", "acoustic guitar"], "A ukulele has four nylon strings, a much smaller body and the tuning G-C-E-A; a guitar has six steel or nylon strings in E-A-D-G-B-E and two and a half octaves more range. The ukulele is the easier first instrument by a wide margin - four strings, soft on the fingers, and most chords need two or three fingers - while a guitar can play bass lines and full chords. A baritone ukulele is the size of a small guitar and is tuned to the guitar's top four strings."),
    (&["vegan", "vegans", "veganism"], &["vegetarian", "vegetarians", "vegetarianism"], "A vegetarian eats no meat or fish but does eat dairy and eggs; a vegan eats nothing that comes from an animal at all, so no milk, cheese, butter, eggs or honey, and no gelatine or leather either. Vegan is the stricter one, so every vegan meal is vegetarian and not the other way round. The one thing to watch on a vegan diet is vitamin B12, which is only in animal food, so it comes from fortified milk and cereals or a supplement."),
    (&["therapy", "therapist", "psychotherapy", "talking therapy", "cbt", "psychologist", "psychologists"], &["counselling", "counseling", "counsellor", "counselor"], "In Britain the words overlap and both mean talking to a trained person, so the honest difference is the shape of the sessions rather than the label. Counselling is usually the listening kind - six to twelve weekly sessions on one problem, such as a bereavement or a decision - while therapy is often longer and follows a method, CBT being the commonest: homework, thought records and a plan you work through. Ask any of them which body they are accredited with, BACP for counsellors and BABCP for CBT, and whether they are on the NHS talking therapies list, where both are free."),
    (&["chromebook", "chromebooks"], &["laptop", "laptops", "windows laptop", "macbook"], "A chromebook runs ChromeOS, which is mostly a browser: it starts in seconds, costs a couple of hundred pounds and gets ten hours on a charge, and most of what it does needs a connection. A laptop runs Windows or macOS, so it can install real programs and play games, and it costs two to four times as much. For browsing, writing and schoolwork a chromebook is enough; for photographs, video editing or games it is not."),
    (&["osteopath", "osteopaths", "osteopathy"], &["chiropractor", "chiropractors", "chiropractic"], "Both treat back and joint pain with their hands and both are regulated - osteopaths by the General Osteopathic Council, chiropractors by the General Chiropractic Council - and neither needs a GP referral. An osteopath works on the whole body with slow stretching and rhythmic pressure, and a chiropractor mostly adjusts the spine, often with the quick movement that cracks. For ordinary low back pain the evidence for both is about the same as for physiotherapy, so pick on the person: a first session is usually 40 to 70 pounds."),
    (&["mushroom", "mushrooms", "field mushroom"], &["toadstool", "toadstools"], "There is no scientific difference: a toadstool is just the old word for a mushroom somebody thought was poisonous, so the two names tell you nothing about whether one is safe to eat. Only the species does, and some of the deadliest in Britain look like ordinary field mushrooms, so pick by species with a book or an expert and never by the word. If someone has eaten an unknown one, keep the remains and ring 111 or the poisons line rather than wait for symptoms."),
    (&["bee", "bees", "bumblebee", "bumblebees", "honeybee", "honeybees"], &["wasp", "wasps", "yellow jacket", "yellowjacket"], "A bee is furry, round and brownish or striped softly, and a honeybee dies after one sting because the sting stays in the skin; a wasp is smooth, bright yellow with black bands and a pinched waist, and it can sting over and over. Bees are after nectar and pollen and leave you alone; wasps hunt insects and in late summer want sugar, which is why they come for your drink. Flick a wasp away rather than swat it, and if a sting brings swelling of the face or trouble breathing, that is 999."),
    (&["pandemic", "pandemics"], &["epidemic", "epidemics"], "An epidemic is more cases of an illness than expected in one place or country; a pandemic is the same thing spread across several countries or continents, so every pandemic starts as an epidemic and most epidemics never become one. The third word is endemic: an illness that is always with us at a steady level, like the common cold or flu in winter. Size makes the difference, not how dangerous the germ is."),
    (&["latte", "lattes", "caffe latte"], &["cappuccino", "cappuccinos"], "Both are espresso and steamed milk, and the difference is the milk: a cappuccino is roughly equal thirds espresso, milk and thick foam in a 150 to 180ml cup, and a latte is mostly milk with a thin layer of foam on top in a 240 to 300ml glass. So a latte is milder and bigger and a cappuccino is foamier and stronger, and a flat white sits between them with two shots and very fine foam."),
    (&["midwife", "midwives", "community midwife"], &["health visitor", "health visitors"], "A midwife looks after you and the baby through pregnancy, the birth and the first 28 days: the appointments, the birth itself, the checks and the feeding help. A health visitor is a nurse or midwife with extra training who takes over at about ten to fourteen days and stays until the child is five, doing the weighing and development checks, the postnatal depression questions and the advice on sleep, food and behaviour. Both are free on the NHS and you do not need to ask for either - they come to you."),
    (&["income tax"], &["national insurance", "ni contributions", "national insurance contributions"], "Income tax is taken from your pay and funds everything the government spends; National Insurance is a separate contribution on your earnings that buys your State Pension and some benefits, so the two lines on a payslip buy different things. NI stops when you reach State Pension age and income tax does not, and only NI needs the qualifying years."),
    (&["moth", "moths"], &["butterfly", "butterflies"], "A butterfly flies by day and rests with its wings folded upright over its back, and its antennae are thin with a club at the tip. A moth mostly flies at night, rests with its wings spread flat or tented, and has feathery or plain antennae - a few, like the hummingbird hawk-moth, fly by day and still read as moths."),
    (&["rabbit", "rabbits", "bunny", "bunnies"], &["hare", "hares"], "A hare is bigger and lankier, with long ears and long back legs, and its young are born furred with their eyes open, in a scrape on the ground, ready to run within hours. A rabbit is smaller and rounder, lives in a burrow in a group, and its young are born blind, bald and helpless. So a leveret sitting still in a field is normal - leave it where it is."),
    (&["bee", "bees", "honeybee", "honeybees", "bumblebee", "bumblebees"], &["wasp", "wasps", "yellow jacket"], "A bee is fuzzy and lives on pollen and nectar, and only defends the nest - a honeybee dies when it stings. A wasp is smooth and bare, with a tight waist and bright yellow bands, and it hunts other insects for its grubs; in late summer, when the grubs are grown, it scavenges anything sweet and stings more, and it can sting again and again."),
    (&["wasp", "wasps", "yellow jacket"], &["hornet", "hornets"], "A hornet is the biggest of the wasp family - a European hornet is 3 to 3.5 cm - and it is brown with orange bands rather than black and yellow. Its sting is no worse venom for venom than a wasp's, there is simply more of it, and it is far less interested in your picnic: it hunts insects at dusk. Sightings of the Asian hornet should go to the app or the number on the Government's page."),
    (&["crow", "crows"], &["raven", "ravens"], "A raven is much bigger - getting on for buzzard size, twice the weight of a carrion crow - with a heavy bill, shaggy throat feathers, a wedge-shaped tail and a deep croaking call. A crow has a fanned tail and a harsh caw, and gathers in flocks in fields and towns, while ravens keep to pairs on moors, cliffs and hills."),
    (&["monkey", "monkeys"], &["ape", "apes"], "An ape has no tail, a broad chest and arms built for hanging from branches, and a bigger brain: gibbons, chimps, gorillas, orangs and people. A monkey usually has a tail and runs along a branch on all fours: baboons, macaques, marmosets. So a chimpanzee is an ape and a baboon is a monkey."),
    (&["seal", "seals"], &["sea lion", "sea lions"], "A sea lion has small ear flaps you can see, long front flippers it can walk on, and it barks; a true seal has no ear flaps, short front flippers, and it hauls itself along on its belly, and it is mostly silent. So the seal on a British beach is a grey or common seal, one of the true seals."),
    (&["rat", "rats"], &["mouse", "mice"], "A rat runs to 20 to 25 cm in the body, with a thick tail as long again, a blunt nose and small ears; a mouse is 7 to 10 cm, with a pointed nose, big ears and a thin tail about as long as its body. Rats travel the same run each night from drains, rubbish and walls; house mice live indoors in cupboards and under the floor."),
    (&["hamster", "hamsters"], &["gerbil", "gerbils"], "A hamster is stocky, lives alone and sleeps most of the day, and two adults in one cage will fight. A gerbil is slimmer with a long tufted tail, lives happily in a pair, and is up and about on and off through the day and night, wanting deep bedding to dig in. So a gerbil wants company and a hamster wants a cage of its own."),
    (&["hen", "hens"], &["chicken", "chickens"], "A chicken is the bird; a hen is an adult female, a pullet is a young female before she starts laying, and a cockerel is the male. Hens lay without a cockerel - those eggs are simply unfertilised - so a household that wants eggs needs hens only."),
    (&["goose", "geese"], &["duck", "ducks"], "A goose is the bigger bird, with a long neck and legs set further back, and it grazes grass; a duck is smaller with a shorter neck, and it dabbles in water for insects and weed. Geese are louder, bolder and better guards, and ducks are calmer and easier in a small garden."),
    (&["sheep"], &["lamb", "lambs"], "A lamb is a sheep under about a year old; a hogget is one to two years, and mutton is older than that. Lamb is milder and quicker to cook, and mutton has more fat and flavour and wants a long, slow pot."),
    (&["koala", "koalas"], &["bear", "bears"], "A koala is not a bear: it is a marsupial, related to the wombat, that carries its joey in a pouch and lives almost entirely on eucalyptus leaves. A bear is a placental meat-eater, so the two are on different branches of the mammals altogether."),
    (&["bison"], &["buffalo", "buffaloes", "water buffalo"], "A bison has a huge shoulder hump, a beard and a shaggy woolly coat, and it lives on the plains of North America and Europe. A buffalo is a different animal - the water buffalo of Asia and the Cape buffalo of Africa - and both carry horns that curve out, with no shoulder hump. The one in the western and on the nickel is a bison."),
    (&["centipede", "centipedes"], &["millipede", "millipedes"], "A centipede has one pair of legs to each segment, a flattened body and real speed: it hunts, and the front pair of legs carries venom. A millipede has two pairs to a segment and a round body, is slow, has no venom, and lives on rotting leaves."),
    (&["llama", "llamas"], &["alpaca", "alpacas"], "An alpaca is the smaller of the two, with a short blunt face and upright, banana-shaped ears, and it is kept for its fine wool. A llama is taller, with a longer head and long ears that curve back, and it is used as a pack animal and as a guard for a flock. Llamas are more independent and will face a fox; alpacas are easily spooked."),
    (&["dolphin", "dolphins"], &["porpoise", "porpoises"], "A dolphin has a long beak, cone-shaped teeth and a curved fin, and it is sociable and noisy. A porpoise is smaller and stouter, with a blunt face, spade-shaped teeth and a small triangular fin, and it is shy and quiet - so the small grey back arcing fast off a British shore, with no show at all, is usually a porpoise."),
    (&["hawk", "hawks"], &["falcon", "falcons"], "A hawk is broad-winged with rounded tips and a plain hooked bill, and it hunts from a perch or a long soar: buzzards, sparrowhawks, goshawks. A falcon has long pointed wings and a notch on the beak, and it hunts in fast level flight or a stoop: kestrels, peregrines, hobbies. So the bird hovering over a verge is a kestrel, a falcon, and the one circling on flat wings with a mewing call is a buzzard, a hawk."),
    (&["sweet potato", "sweet potatoes"], &["yam", "yams"], "Sweet potatoes are the orange- or cream-fleshed roots in most supermarkets; true yams are the big dark roots with bark-like skin sold in African and Caribbean grocers, and they need peeling and a long cook. In America an orange 'yam' is usually a sweet potato under another name, so cook what you bought as you would a sweet potato."),
    (&["courgette", "courgettes", "zucchini", "zucchinis"], &["cucumber", "cucumbers"], "A courgette comes from the marrow family and is cooked: soft edible skin, spongy seedy middle, and it softens in the pan. A cucumber is watery and eaten raw, usually peeled or scored, and it turns to mush if you cook it. Small ones look alike; the taste does not."),
    (&["clementine", "clementines", "satsuma", "satsumas", "tangerine", "tangerines", "mandarin", "mandarins"], &["lemon", "lemons", "orange", "oranges", "grapefruit"], "All the easy peelers are mandarins: a satsuma is the earliest, with the loosest skin and usually no pips, a clementine is smaller and sweeter and sometimes has a few, and a tangerine is deeper orange with a stronger taste. Sweet and easy to peel means a satsuma or clementine, so pick one that feels light for its size - and a lemon, an orange or a grapefruit is none of them."),
    (&["clementine", "clementines", "tangerine", "tangerines", "mandarin", "mandarins"], &["satsuma", "satsumas"], "A satsuma is the loosest-skinned easy peeler and usually has no pips, and it is the earliest of them in the shops. A clementine is smaller and sweeter with a tighter skin, and it sometimes has a few pips. Both are mandarins, so buy whichever feels light for its size."),
    (&["latte", "lattes"], &["flat white", "flat whites"], "A latte is a shot of espresso with a lot of steamed milk, around 220ml in a cafe mug, so it is mild and milky. A flat white is the same shot with far less milk, about 130ml, in a smaller cup, so the coffee tastes stronger. If you want the coffee to taste of coffee, order the flat white."),
    (&["lease", "leases"], &["tenancy", "tenancies", "tenancy agreement"], "In Scotland a lease is what the rest of the UK calls a tenancy: the written agreement that sets the rent and the notice. In England and Wales the usual one is an assured shorthold tenancy, and a lease is the long agreement you buy when you own a flat. So a lease in Scotland is short and a lease in England is long."),
    (&["jam", "jams"], &["jelly", "jellies", "preserve", "preserves"], "In Britain jam has the fruit in it and jelly is the clear set made from strained juice, and in America 'jelly' is that clear set while 'jam' is the British jam. Marmalade is citrus with shreds of peel, and a preserve is the chunky one with whole or large pieces of fruit. So a US recipe that says jelly wants the smooth, clear set."),
    (&["griddle", "griddles", "griddle pan"], &["frying pan", "frying pans", "fry pan"], "A griddle is flat, or ridged, and heavy, with low or no sides: a plate of heat for pancakes, bacon and several chops at once, and fat runs off a ridged one. A frying pan has deep sloping sides, so things can be tossed in it and the fat or sauce stays in."),
    (&["slow cooker", "slow cookers"], &["pressure cooker", "pressure cookers"], "A slow cooker cooks on low for six to eight hours and can be left all day; a pressure cooker seals the steam in and does the same work in a third of the time or less, under pressure. Beans, tough cuts and stock are far quicker in the pressure cooker, and a stew you want waiting when you get in is the slow cooker's."),
    (&["broil", "broils", "broiling", "broiled"], &["grill", "grills", "grilling", "grilled"], "In Britain the grill is the heat from above and a barbecue is over the coals; in America 'broil' is the heat from above and 'grill' is over the coals. So a US recipe that says broil means the grill pan under the element, and where an American says grill, light the barbecue."),
    (&["jumper", "jumpers"], &["sweater", "sweaters"], "Nothing: a jumper and a sweater are the same knitted top, jumper being the British word and sweater the American one. A cardigan is the one that opens down the front, and a pullover is any of them you put on over your head."),
    (&["trainer", "trainers"], &["sneaker", "sneakers"], "Nothing: a trainer is the British word for a sneaker, and both are the same soft sports shoe. Plimsolls are the flat canvas ones, and 'runners' is what Americans call the sort you run in."),
    (&["biscuit", "biscuits"], &["cookie", "cookies"], "In Britain a biscuit is the crisp flat sort - digestives, rich tea - and a cookie is the soft, chunky American-style one. In America both are cookies, and an American biscuit is a soft, scone-like bread eaten with gravy. So a British recipe for cookies wants the chewy kind."),
    (&["savings account", "savings accounts"], &["isa", "isas", "cash isa", "cash isas"], "Interest in a cash ISA is free of tax; interest in an ordinary savings account is taxed once it is past your personal savings allowance - £1,000 a year at basic rate, £500 at higher rate, nothing at additional rate. So if the rates are close the ISA wins, and if the ordinary account pays much more, do the sum."),
    (&["loan", "loans"], &["overdraft", "overdrafts"], "An overdraft is borrowing on your current account, meant for days rather than months: it is charged by the day or the month and the bank can withdraw it. A loan is a fixed sum paid back over an agreed term at an agreed rate. So a few days over is an overdraft, and anything over a month or two is usually cheaper as a loan."),
    (&["tenant", "tenants"], &["lodger", "lodgers"], "A tenant rents a whole home and has a tenancy, so the landlord needs a court order to evict them and the notice the law sets. A lodger lives in the same home as the owner, sharing the kitchen and bathroom, and can be asked to leave on reasonable notice - usually the rent period. So the spare room in your own house holds a lodger, and the flat next door a tenant."),
    (&["supervisor", "supervisors"], &["manager", "managers"], "A supervisor runs the shift: what gets done today, by whom, and to what standard. A manager carries the budget, the hiring, the targets and the reviews. Titles vary from one employer to the next, so the tell is who spends the money and who decides the people."),
    (&["loft", "lofts"], &["attic", "attics"], "The same space under the roof: a loft is the bare one holding the boxes, and an attic is that space made into a room. In Britain the job is a loft conversion; in America it is an attic room."),
    (&["patio", "patios"], &["deck", "decks", "decking"], "A patio is paving or concrete laid on the ground; a deck is timber boards on a raised frame. A patio is cheaper, lasts decades with little care and stays cool underfoot; a deck goes down faster on uneven ground, is warmer barefoot and needs treating and cleaning every year or two."),
    (&["shrub", "shrubs"], &["bush", "bushes"], "Nothing: a shrub is the gardener's word for a bush, a woody plant with several stems from the base rather than one trunk. If the stems meet the ground in a cluster it is a shrub; a tree has a single trunk."),
    (&["statement", "statements"], &["receipt", "receipts"], "A receipt is the proof of one purchase, and it is what a shop asks for if you want a refund or a swap. A bank statement lists everything that went in and out of an account over a month. Keep the receipt for anything you might return, and the statement is for checking what left your account."),
    (&["cv", "cvs", "resume", "resumes"], &["cover letter", "covering letter", "covering letters", "cover letters"], "A CV is the one- or two-page record of what you have done - jobs, dates, qualifications - and it is the same document for every application. A cover letter is the short note written for that one job: why you want it and what you would bring. The CV carries the facts and the letter makes the case."),
    (&["psychiatrist", "psychiatrists"], &["psychologist", "psychologists"], "A psychiatrist is a medical doctor who can diagnose mental illness and prescribe medicine; a psychologist is trained in psychology and works with talking therapy and testing, and cannot prescribe. In Britain a GP refers you to a psychiatrist, and you can reach a psychologist through the NHS or privately."),
    (&["novella", "novellas"], &["novel", "novels"], "A novel is the long one, usually over 50,000 words; a novella runs from about 17,500 to 40,000, long enough for a chapter of depth and short enough to read in an evening. Under about 17,500 it is a long short story. Publishers are loose about the line; for a reader, judge by whether it takes a sitting."),
    (&["autobiography", "autobiographies"], &["biography", "biographies"], "An autobiography is a life written by the person who lived it, and a biography is written by somebody else. A memoir is the autobiography's selective cousin: the part of a life that bears on one theme."),
    (&["cold sore", "cold sores"], &["ulcer", "ulcers"], "A cold sore is the herpes virus: a cluster of small blisters on a tingle, usually at the edge of the lip, which crusts over in a few days and comes back in the same spot when you are run down. A mouth ulcer is a raw pale patch inside the mouth, on the tongue, cheek or gum - not blistered and not catching - and it usually heals in a week or two. A cold sore is worth a pharmacist's cream in the first day or so; an ulcer that lasts past three weeks is worth showing to a dentist."),
    (&["stock cube", "stock cubes"], &["bouillon cube", "bouillon cubes", "bouillon"], "Nothing much: bouillon is French for stock, and a bouillon cube is the same cube of salt, yeast extract and flavourings in a different wrapper. Powder, paste and jelly pots do the same job. What matters is the salt - taste before you season - and whether the stock is the point of the dish, because fresh stock beats a cube when it is."),
    (&["fridge", "fridges", "refrigerator"], &["freezer", "freezers"], "A fridge runs at 0 to 5C, cold enough to slow the bugs that spoil food: milk keeps for days and cooked food for a few. A freezer runs at -18C, where almost nothing grows, so food keeps for months - but freezing does not kill the bugs, it only parks them, so it still has to be cooked properly after it thaws."),
    (&["tune", "tunes", "melody", "melodies"], &["song", "songs"], "A tune is the melody on its own, the bit you hum; a song has words and a voice on top of the tune. So an instrumental has a tune but is not a song, and something the words carry - a rap, a hymn sung flat - is still a song."),
    (&["shallow fry", "shallow frying", "shallow-fry", "shallow-frying"], &["deep fry", "deep frying", "deep-fry", "deep-frying"], "Shallow frying is a centimetre or two of oil in a pan, with the food turned once; deep frying covers the food, so it crisps all over and cooks quickly. Deep frying wants more oil, a thermometer and a lid, and 170 to 190C - too cool and the food drinks the oil, too hot and the outside burns before the middle is done."),
    (&["puppy", "puppies", "pup", "pups"], &["dog", "dogs"], "A puppy is a dog under about a year, and the difference that matters is what it can do: a puppy eats food built for growth, three or four small meals a day, and needs short play and long sleeps. An adult dog eats twice a day and can run for an hour. Big breeds are still growing at 18 months, so a large puppy at a year is still a puppy."),
    (&["kitten", "kittens"], &["cat", "cats"], "A kitten is a cat under about a year: it needs kitten food with more protein and fat, several small meals, warmth and company, and everything is still being learned - scratch posts, short gentle play, and one room at a time in a new house. A grown cat wants less food and less fuss, and it sleeps two thirds of the day."),
    (&["magpie", "magpies"], &["crow", "crows", "raven", "ravens"], "A magpie is a corvid the size of a small crow, black and white with a long wedge of a tail; a crow is all black, bigger, and gathers in flocks in fields. Both are in the crow family, with the jay, the jackdaw and the rook."),
    (&["syrup", "syrups"], &["sauce", "sauces"], "A syrup is sugar dissolved in water - golden, maple, treacle - thin and pourable; a sauce is thickened to coat, with flour, cornflour, egg or cream, and it may not be sweet at all. A syrup is poured over and soaks in; a sauce sits on top."),
    (&["contract", "contracts"], &["agreement", "agreements"], "An agreement is any two people saying yes to the same thing; a contract is an agreement the law will enforce, which needs an offer, an acceptance and something of value each way - and no signature at all, since a yes by email or a deposit paid can be enough. So every contract is an agreement, and most agreements are not contracts."),
    (&["credit score", "credit scores"], &["credit report", "credit reports", "credit file", "credit files"], "The report is the record: every account, payment and search in your name, kept by Experian, Equifax and TransUnion. The score is a number one of those agencies works out from the report. So you fix the report - correct the errors, register to vote, pay on time, keep balances low - and the score follows."),
    (&["meeting", "meetings"], &["catch up", "catch-up", "catchup", "catch ups"], "A meeting has an agenda and a decision at the end; a catch-up is a short, informal check-in with no decision to make, usually one to one. Call a meeting when something has to be agreed and a catch-up when you only need to stay in touch - and say which it is in the invitation."),
    (&["hedge", "hedges"], &["fence", "fences"], "A hedge is living, so it wants clipping once or twice a year and it dies if it is not watered in a drought; a fence is built, wants treating every few years and will need replacing in 15 to 20. A hedge gives birds cover and slows the wind; a fence goes up in a weekend, costs less and takes up no ground."),
    (&["squirrel", "squirrels"], &["chipmunk", "chipmunks"], "A chipmunk is much smaller - about 15 cm in the body, with a short tail - and it has stripes down its face and back and lives on the ground in a burrow in North America. A squirrel is bigger, with a long bushy tail and no face stripes, and it climbs. So the striped thing in a British garden is a grey squirrel in good light, not a chipmunk."),
    (&["lizard", "lizards"], &["gecko", "geckos"], "A gecko is one family of lizard: soft skin with tiny scales, no eyelids on most kinds (they lick the eye clean), toe pads that grip glass, and a chirp. The lizard in a British garden is usually a common lizard or a slow worm - dry scales, eyelids, and no voice at all."),
    (&["career", "careers"], &["job", "jobs"], "A job is what you are paid to do now, with a contract and a wage; a career is the run of them and the direction - the skills, the reputation, the next step. So changing jobs can be no change of career at all, and a career change often starts with a pay cut."),
    (&["bungalow", "bungalows"], &["house", "houses"], "A bungalow is a house of one storey, so there are no stairs; house covers any of them. In Britain the word usually means a single-storey home with its own front door, and it is priced and built differently: easier to live in with a wheelchair, and with more roof to extend into."),
    (&["nurse", "nurses"], &["carer", "carers"], "A nurse is registered with the nursing council and does the clinical work - medicines, wounds, observations - in a hospital, surgery or care home. A carer helps with daily living: washing, dressing, meals and company, and needs no registration, whether it is a paid care worker or a relative doing it unpaid. So a health decision is the nurse's, and the help through the day is the carer's."),
    (&["boiler", "boilers"], &["furnace", "furnaces"], "A boiler heats water and sends it round the radiators and the taps. A furnace heats air and blows it through ducts, which is the usual American heating; an old British back boiler is a boiler behind a fire. So a boiler has a flow temperature that matters and a furnace has a fan - the same job done through a different pipe."),
    (&["sprain", "sprains"], &["break", "breaks", "fracture", "fractures"], "A sprain is a stretched or torn ligament at a joint, and a break is bone, which hurts over the bone itself and swells. You cannot tell them apart by how much it hurts, so the test is what you can do: if you cannot put weight on it, it is bent oddly, or you cannot use the limb, get it looked at the same day and let them X-ray it."),
    (&["pond", "ponds"], &["pool", "pools", "swimming pool", "swimming pools"], "A pond is a small still body of water, in a garden or a field, with plants, mud and wildlife, and nothing keeps it clean but the plants and the balance. A pool is built and filtered for swimming, so it needs a pump, chemicals and a cover. So a pond wants the shallow edge and the frogs, and a pool wants the filter."),
    (&["rabbit", "rabbits"], &["guinea pig", "guinea pigs", "cavy", "cavies"], "A guinea pig is the smaller, rounder one, with no tail and short legs, and it cannot make its own vitamin C, so it needs it in the food or in a supplement. A rabbit is bigger, with long ears and back legs, and it needs a run it can stand up in and dig. Guinea pigs want company of their own kind; a rabbit is strong enough to hurt one by accident, so a pair of rabbits or a pair of guinea pigs, not one of each."),
    (&["fine", "fines"], &["penalty", "penalties"], "A fine is money a court or a council orders you to pay for breaking a rule. A penalty is the wider word: it is any punishment or extra charge - a penalty fare on the train, a penalty charge notice on the car, a late-payment penalty on a bill. So every fine is a penalty, and most penalties are not fines."),
];
