const MATERIAL_PAINT_WITH_NO_SIZE: &str = "One litre of emulsion covers about 10 to 12 square metres, and you need two coats, so count the wall area and double it: add the walls up as perimeter times ceiling height (a 4m by 4m room with 2.4m ceilings is about 38 square metres), take off roughly 1.7 square metres for a door and 1.2 for a window, and that room wants about 7 litres - two 5 litre tins, or a 10 to leave some for touching up. Ceilings and a second colour are extra, and a dark colour over a light wall often takes three coats.";
const MATERIAL_WALLPAPER_WITH_NO_SIZE: &str = "Count drops, not floor: divide the room's perimeter by the 52cm width of a standard roll and round up (a 4m by 4m room is 16m round, so 31 drops), then divide a roll's 10m by the ceiling height plus the pattern repeat to see how many drops each gives - usually three - and 31 drops is about 11 rolls. Buy one extra for a mistake and matching, and keep the batch number the same on every roll.";
const JOINT_TIMELINE_REPLY: &str = "Walking on it the day after the operation, off the sticks at about 6 weeks, driving again at 6 weeks once you can do an emergency stop without thinking, and back to desk work at 6 to 12 weeks: hospital is usually 2 to 4 nights for a hip and 1 to 3 for a knee, and full recovery is 6 to 12 months for a hip and up to a year for a knee. Kneeling after a knee replacement is uncomfortable for most people and may never come back fully. Ring the ward or 111 if the wound starts leaking, the joint turns hot or red, or the pain suddenly gets worse.";
const DIET_DAIRY_FREE_VEGGIE: &str = "Vegetarian and no dairy, which is one list and not two: a chickpea and spinach curry with rice, a lentil and tomato lasagne made with oil instead of the cheese sauce, or a tray of roasted veg with chickpeas and couscous. Watch the labels for whey, casein and milk powder, which sit in stock cubes, bread and biscuits.";
const DIET_VEGETARIAN_MENU: &str = "Vegetarian and still a proper dinner: a chickpea and spinach curry with rice, a lentil and mushroom lasagne, or a tray of roasted veg with feta and couscous. One dish everyone eats beats a meat main with something made separately.";
const DIET_VEGAN_MENU: &str = "Vegan and still a proper dinner: a chickpea and spinach curry with rice, a lentil and tomato lasagne with oil in place of the cheese sauce, or a tray of roasted veg with chickpeas and couscous. Check the stock cubes, the bread and the pesto, which are where the milk usually hides.";
const DIET_VEGETARIAN_SWAPS: &str = "Nothing has to change about the amounts: swap the meat for a fist of chickpeas, lentils, eggs or cheese, and keep the vegetables, rice or potatoes as they are.";
const DIET_VEGAN_SWAPS: &str = "Nothing has to change about the amounts: swap the meat for a fist of chickpeas, lentils, tofu or nuts, use oil or a plant spread instead of butter, and keep the vegetables and rice or potatoes. Check the stock cubes, the bread and the pesto, where the milk usually hides.";
const OPINION_GADGET_VERDICT: &str = "The honest test is whether it earns its counter space: three uses a week and it was a good buy, once a month and it lives in a cupboard. Give it a month of proper use before you decide, and keep one recipe you like so it is not a project every time.";
const SALES_CALENDAR: &str = "The big retail sales are the January sales (from Boxing Day to the end of January), the Black Friday week at the end of November, the Easter and August bank holiday weekends, and the end-of-season clearances - summer things in August, winter things in January and February. January and Black Friday are the deepest cuts, and the item you want most is the one worth waiting for rather than buying on impulse at a weaker sale.";
const LOCATION_ACCESS: &str = "Check which people and apps have location access. On an iPhone, review Find My > People or Settings > Privacy & Security > Safety Check. In Google Maps, check your profile picture > Location sharing. In WhatsApp, check Settings > Privacy > Live location. If changing anything could put you at risk, consider using a safe device to plan your next step.";
const SPORT_CALF_STRETCH: &str = "For the calf, stand with both hands on a wall, one leg back with the heel flat on the floor and the back knee straight, and lean in until you feel it pull: hold 30 seconds, three times each side. Then bend the back knee slightly, keeping the heel down, for the lower calf. Stretch after you run rather than before, when the muscle is warm, and breathe out into the stretch instead of bouncing.";
const SPORT_HAMSTRING_STRETCH: &str = "For the hamstring, sit or stand with one heel on a low step, keep that leg straight and hinge forward from the hips with a flat back until you feel the pull behind the thigh - hold 30 seconds, three times each side. No bouncing and no reaching for your toes with a round back. Do it after the run, when the muscle is warm.";
const SPORT_QUAD_STRETCH: &str = "For the front of the thigh, the quad: stand tall, take one foot behind you and pull the heel up towards your bottom with the knee pointing down at the floor and your hips level, and hold 30 seconds each side - a hand on a wall or a chair for balance. If the pull is at the back of the thigh instead, that is the hamstring: heel on a low step, leg straight, hinge forward from the hips with a flat back, 30 seconds each side. Both after the exercise, when the muscle is warm.";
const SPORT_LEG_STRETCH: &str = "For the whole leg, the calf first: hands on a wall, one leg back with the heel flat on the floor and that knee straight, lean in for 30 seconds, three times each side. Then the hamstring: one heel on a low step, that leg straight, hinge forward from the hips with a flat back, 30 seconds each side. Both after the exercise, when the muscles are warm, not before.";
const PASTA_NOODLES_REPLY: &str = "Noodles are quick but not all the same: thin or instant ones are 3 to 4 minutes, egg noodles 4 to 5, and dried rice noodles 6 to 8, so check the packet for yours.";
const MEASURE_COFFEE_RATIO: &str = "two level tablespoons of ground coffee per 200ml mug, which is about 12g, so 60g fills a litre jug and 240g makes 20 mugs";
const PET_COST_RESCUE_LINE: &str = "From a rescue it is about 150 to 350 pounds for a puppy or a dog, which usually covers the neutering, the microchip and the first jabs. The first year still costs more than that: insurance 20 to 40 a month, food, worming, a crate and a bed. Budget around 1,000 in the first year and keep a vet fund separately.";
const PET_COST_BREEDER_LINE: &str = "A pedigree puppy is about 800 to 3,000 pounds depending on the breed, and the first year costs more than the puppy does: jabs and a microchip 60 to 100, neutering 150 to 300, insurance 20 to 40 a month, plus food, worming, a crate and a bed. Budget around 1,500 in the first year and keep a vet fund separately. Walk away from anyone who will not show you the mother.";
const PET_COST_KITTEN_LINE: &str = "A kitten is about 50 to 150 pounds from a rescue or a friend's litter, and 300 to 800 for a pedigree; the first year costs more than the kitten does: jabs and a microchip 60 to 100, neutering 60 to 120 for a male and more for a female, insurance 8 to 20 a month, plus litter, food and a carrier. Budget around 800 in the first year and keep a vet fund separately.";
const PET_COST_PUPPY_LINE: &str = "A puppy is about 800 to 3,000 pounds from a breeder depending on the breed, and 150 to 350 from a rescue, and the first year costs more than the puppy does: jabs and a microchip 60 to 100, neutering 150 to 300, insurance 20 to 40 a month, plus food, worming, a crate and a bed. Budget around 1,500 in the first year and keep a vet fund separately. Walk away from anyone who will not show you the mother.";
const PET_COST_COMPARE_LINE: &str = "A cat is the cheaper of the two to keep: about 800 to 1,200 pounds a year against 1,000 to 1,700 for a dog, because insurance is 8 to 20 a month instead of 20 to 40 and a cat eats far less than a medium dog. The buying price is the other way round - a pedigree puppy is usually dearer than a pedigree kitten - but over ten years the dog costs the most.";
const SMALL_PET_RABBIT_LINE: &str = "A rabbit is 150 to 300 pounds a year to keep, and the set-up is the real money: a hutch with a run attached, or a shed, is 200 to 400, then hay all day at 20 to 40 a month, pellets, a few greens, neutering 60 to 120 and the annual jabs. Keep two, because a rabbit alone is a stressed rabbit, and they live eight to ten years.";
const SMALL_PET_HAMSTER_LINE: &str = "A hamster is 10 to 20 pounds to buy and 5 to 10 a month to keep: bedding, food and a little veg. The cage is the cost - 60 to 120 pounds for a tank at least 100 by 50cm with a deep base to dig in, not the small barred cage in the shop. Save for the vet too: not every practice treats small furries, and a consultation is 30 to 60 pounds.";
const SMALL_PET_GUINEA_LINE: &str = "A guinea pig is 10 to 25 pounds each and 200 to 400 a year to keep: a cage at least 120 by 60cm, hay all day, pellets with added vitamin C, and greens every day. They need a friend of their own kind, so two is the real minimum, and they live five to seven years.";
const SMALL_PET_BUDGIE_LINE: &str = "A budgie is 15 to 40 pounds and about 150 to 250 a year: a wide cage rather than a tall one, at least 60cm across so it can fly between perches, a friend, and pellets with daily veg. The catch is the vet - an avian vet charges more than a small-animal practice, and a budgie should see one at the first sign of fluffing up.";
const SMALL_PET_HORSE_LINE: &str = "A horse costs 4,000 to 8,000 pounds a year all in: livery at 150 to 400 a month, hay and feed, shoes every six weeks at 80 to 120, insurance, worming, teeth and the vet. The horse itself is the cheap part. Never buy one without a vetting and a trial, and budget the first year higher still for tack and rugs.";
const SMALL_PET_FISH_LINE: &str = "Setting up a tank is 150 to 300 pounds - tank, filter, heater, gravel, a test kit and water treatment - and then 10 to 20 a month for food and electricity. Cycle the tank for four to six weeks before any fish go in, and start with a few hardy ones: most first tanks fail because the fish go in on day one.";
const JOINT_SHOULDER_TIMELINE_REPLY: &str = "A shoulder replacement is in a sling for the first 3 to 6 weeks, with gentle movement exercises started within days and the physio's programme once the sling comes off: driving again at about 6 to 8 weeks once you can turn the wheel and brake without pain, desk work at 6 to 12 weeks, and full recovery 6 to 12 months. Hospital is usually one or two nights. Ring the ward or 111 if the wound starts leaking, the shoulder turns hot or red, or the pain suddenly gets worse.";
const JOINT_IMPLANT_REPLY: &str = "A hip or knee replacement usually lasts 15 to 25 years, and about 8 in 10 are still going at 20 years; when one does wear out, a second operation to replace the worn parts is a routine job - smaller than the first, with a similar recovery. It is not a countdown: weight, activity and the wear on it decide, so the muscles kept strong and a healthy weight are what buy the time. If a joint that has been fine for years starts to ache, grind or give way, that is worth an X-ray rather than waiting.";
const JOINT_SHOULDER_IMPLANT_REPLY: &str = "A shoulder replacement usually lasts 10 to 15 years, and a reverse shoulder about the same - it is not a countdown: the muscle you keep working and the load you put through it decide, so the exercises the physio gives you are what buy the time. If a shoulder that has been fine for years starts to ache, grind or give way, that is worth an X-ray rather than waiting, and a worn shoulder can be revised the way a hip can.";
const JOINT_SORE_REPLY: &str = "Yes - soreness and stiffness for months after a joint replacement is normal, and it eases with the exercises rather than with rest: the joint is at its stiffest in the first six weeks and keeps loosening for up to a year, and a warm, puffy knee or hip after exercise is the usual reaction to doing more. Build up slowly, ice it for 15 minutes after the exercises, and keep the daily ones going even on a bad day. Ordinary swelling is around the joint; a calf or a whole leg that swells, goes hot or is tender to touch is not ordinary swelling - ring the ward or 111 the same day, because a clot is the thing to rule out. Ring about the joint too if it is hot and red, the wound leaks, or the pain gets worse each week rather than better.";
const JOINT_SHOULDER_SORE_REPLY: &str = "Yes - soreness and stiffness for months after a shoulder replacement is normal, and it eases with the exercises rather than with rest: it is at its stiffest in the first six weeks and keeps loosening for up to a year, and a warm, puffy shoulder after a session is the usual reaction to doing more. Build up slowly, ice it for 15 minutes after the exercises, and keep the daily ones going even on a bad day. Ring the ward or 111 if the shoulder is hot and red, the wound leaks, or the pain gets worse each week rather than better.";
const JOINT_SLEEPING_REPLY: &str = "Sleep is the awkward part for the first few weeks, and the answer depends on the joint: after a hip, sleep on your back with a pillow between the knees, or on the unoperated side with the pillow still between the knees so the new hip cannot cross the middle - keep that up for the six weeks the ward tells you. After a knee, sleep on your back with the leg straight on a pillow, or on the good side with a pillow under the new knee. A pillow under the heels stops the sheet rubbing, and a bad night is normal rather than a sign of damage.";
const JOINT_SHOULDER_SLEEPING_REPLY: &str = "Sleep is the awkward part for the first weeks after a shoulder replacement: sleep propped up on two or three pillows with the arm supported on one in front of you or on a pillow beside you, and not on the operated side. A cushion tucked under the elbow takes the pull off the shoulder, and most people find a recliner or the corner of a sofa easier than a bed at first. A bad night is normal rather than a sign of damage.";
const JOINT_LOCK_REPLY: &str = "A {part} that locks, catches or will not straighten has something physically in the way, so it is worth being examined this week rather than waiting for it to settle: ring your GP and say which movement brings it on, whether it lets go on its own, and whether there was a twist or a knock before it started. A knee that locks is usually a torn cartilage or a loose piece of it catching in the joint, which a physio or doctor can tell apart by hand in one appointment, so keep off twisting sports and deep squats until then rather than testing it. Go to 111 the same day if the joint swells up, is hot, or you cannot put weight on it at all.";
const MEDICINE_TABLET_TEXT: &str = "A tablet that has not helped in the time it should is worth checking before you take more: read the dose and how often it says on the box, and do not add a second thing with the same ingredient (paracetamol is hidden in a lot of cold remedies). A pharmacist will go through everything you take together, for free, and say what is safe to add. If it is a prescribed medicine that is not working, tell the doctor who prescribed it rather than stopping it or doubling it yourself.";
const BODY_TRIAGE_WAX_REPLY: &str = "Waxing wants hair about 5mm long, a patch test a day ahead and one firm pull against the growth with the skin held tight; take an antihistamine an hour before if you come up blotchy. Shaving is quicker: end of a warm shower, fresh blade, conditioner instead of foam, and with the hair rather than against it, which is what keeps the red bumps away. An ingrown hair wants a warm compress and an exfoliating glove every other day.";
const BODY_TRIAGE_SHAVE_REPLY: &str = "Shave at the end of a warm shower with a fresh blade and conditioner, rinsing the blade often, and on legs and bikini line go with the hair, not against it, which is what keeps the red bumps away. Waxing wants hair 5mm long, a patch test a day ahead and a firm pull against the growth, plus an antihistamine an hour before if you come up blotchy. An ingrown hair wants a warm compress and an exfoliating glove every other day.";
const FAMILY_QUIET_FRIEND_REPLY: &str = "Send one small, specific message - a photo with a line on it ('saw this and thought of you - how are you doing?'), or a question only they would know the answer to - and then leave it. {reason}One warm message is easy to answer where three is a job. If there is no reply, wait a month, then try once more and let it rest; a friendship that needs chasing that often is telling you something.";
const FAMILY_QUIET_MOVED_REASON: &str = "A friend who has gone quiet after you moved is usually out of the loop rather than upset. ";
const FAMILY_QUIET_REASON: &str = "Most quiet stretches are a busy month or something they have not said, not a verdict on you. ";
const JOINT_TRIGGER_FINGER_REPLY: &str = "A finger that locks or clicks and then catches as it straightens is trigger finger: the tendon that bends the finger runs through a sheath, the sheath swells, and the tendon has to jump through the tight spot - which is why it locks bent and then lets go with a click. Rest it from hard gripping for a few weeks, keep it straight at night with a finger splint from a chemist, and take ibuprofen if it is sore. See your GP if it stays locked, keeps locking during the day, or a second finger starts: a steroid injection into the sheath usually sorts it, and a small operation does if it does not.";
