    pub(super) const STAPLES: &[(&[&str], &str)] = &[
        (&["brown rice"], "Brown rice wants more water and longer than white: one mug of rice to two mugs of water, lid on, 25 to 30 minutes on the lowest heat. Then 10 minutes off the heat before you fork it through. Rinse it first and don't stir it."),
        (&["quinoa"], "Quinoa takes 12 to 15 minutes: one part quinoa to two of water, lid on, simmering, then 5 minutes off the heat and fork it through. Rinse it first, or it tastes soapy."),
        (&["couscous"], "Couscous needs no cooking: same volume of boiling water or stock as couscous, cover the bowl and leave it 5 minutes, then fork it through with a knob of butter and a squeeze of lemon so it stays loose."),
        (&["barley", "pearl barley"], "Pearl barley takes 25 to 30 minutes simmered in three times its volume of water or stock, and whole barley 45 to 60. It is done when a grain squashes between your fingers with no chalky middle, so taste it rather than time it."),
        (&["bulgur", "bulgur wheat"], "Bulgur wheat takes 10 to 12 minutes simmered in twice its volume of water, or pour boiling stock over it, cover and leave it 20 minutes off the heat. Fork it through with a little olive oil."),
        (&["wild rice"], "Wild rice is a grass seed rather than a rice, so it takes 40 to 45 minutes in plenty of boiling water - about three parts water to one of rice - until the grains split and taste nutty. Drain it and rinse off the starch."),
        (&["rice cooker"], "Rinse the rice first, then the same one mug of rice to one and a half mugs of water, level off with the back of a finger and don't go past the line inside the bowl. Let it finish on its own, leave it on warm for 10 minutes, then fork it through."),
        (&["rice", "basmati", "long grain rice", "white rice", "jasmine rice", "rice cooker"], "One mug of rice to one and a half mugs of water, lid on, 10 to 12 minutes on the lowest heat, then 10 minutes off the heat with the lid still on before you fork it through. Rinse it until the water runs clear first. Don't stir it and don't drain it - that is what makes it gluey."),
        (&["porridge", "oats", "oatmeal", "porridge oats"], "50g of oats to 300ml of milk or water per person, with a pinch of salt, stirred over a medium heat for 4 to 5 minutes. Take it off while it still looks a little loose, because it thickens as it stands, and stir in a splash more milk if it sets."),
        (&["white sauce", "cheese sauce", "bechamel", "béchamel"], "About 10 minutes start to finish. Melt 50g of butter, stir in 50g of plain flour and cook that for a minute, then add 500ml of milk a good splash at a time, whisking each one in before the next goes in. Simmer it for 5 minutes until it coats the back of a spoon, then season it. For a cheese sauce, stir in 100g of grated cheddar once it is off the heat."),
        (&["mash", "mashed potato"], "Use a floury potato like Maris Piper or King Edward, cut into even chunks and boiled in salted water for 15 to 20 minutes. Drain them, leave them in the colander for a minute to steam dry, then mash with butter and warm milk - cold milk makes it gluey - and don't use a food processor."),
        (&["vegetables", "veg"], "The one rule for vegetables is short and hot: steam or boil them 5 to 7 minutes so they still have a bite, or roast them at 200C (180C fan) for 25 to 35 minutes in oil with salt. Cut them to a similar size and they finish together, and salt the water rather than the pan."),
        (&["chop an onion", "chop onions", "chopping an onion", "chopping onions", "dice an onion", "dice onions", "dicing an onion", "dice onions", "slice an onion", "cut an onion", "cut up an onion", "prepare an onion"], "Halve it top to bottom through the root, lay a half cut side down and slice across it towards the root, then cut the slices the other way, and it is diced. Leave the root end on until the last cut, because it holds the layers together. Use the sharpest knife you have and keep the tip on the board: a blunt knife crushes the cells and releases more of the sulphur that makes your eyes water."),
        (&["peel a potato", "peel potatoes", "peeling potatoes", "peel a potato quickly", "how do i peel potatoes", "prepare potatoes", "prepare a potato"], "Take the skin off in strips with a peeler, working away from you and turning the potato as you go, then dig any eyes out with the tip of the peeler. Drop them into a bowl of cold water if they are not going in the pan straight away, because peeled potato goes pink in the air. Peel floury ones like Maris Piper just before they cook, not the night before."),
        (&["chop garlic", "crush garlic", "mince garlic", "chop a garlic clove", "prepare garlic", "chop garlic cloves", "peel a garlic clove", "peel garlic cloves"], "Lay the clove on the board, put the flat of the knife on top and press down with the heel of your hand: the skin slips off and the clove is already crushed. Chop it roughly, sprinkle a pinch of salt on it and drag the flat of the knife over it in circles to turn it into a paste. Crushed like that it sweetens in the pan; left in chunks it burns."),
        (&["peel ginger", "chop ginger", "grate ginger", "prepare ginger", "mince ginger"], "Scrape the skin off with the edge of a teaspoon, which follows the knobs better than a peeler and wastes less of the root. Grate it on the fine side of a grater, or freeze the knob whole and grate it from frozen, which is quicker and keeps for months. The skin is thin and edible, so it can stay on if it is going into a curry."),
        (&["chop a carrot", "chop carrots", "dice a carrot", "dice carrots", "slice a carrot", "slice carrots", "prepare carrots", "prepare a carrot"], "Top and tail it, cut it in half lengthways and lay the flat side down so it cannot roll. For sticks, cut each half into lengths and slice those; for dice, cut the lengths into batons first, then turn them and slice across. Keep every piece the same size, because that is what makes them cook at the same rate."),
        (&["chop mushrooms", "slice mushrooms", "prepare mushrooms", "cleaning mushrooms", "cleaning a mushroom"], "Wipe the caps with damp kitchen paper, or rinse them quickly under the tap and dry them on a tea towel - either works, because a few seconds of water adds nothing a mushroom did not already have, and only a bowl of soaking water makes them watery. Snap the stalk out level with the cap and slice straight down through cap and stalk together. Do not crowd the pan - a crowded pan of mushrooms boils in its own water and never browns."),
        (&["cut an avocado", "prepare an avocado", "peel an avocado", "stone an avocado", "remove the stone from an avocado", "slice an avocado", "how do i cut an avocado"], "Run the knife round the stone from top to bottom, twist the two halves apart, then lever the stone out with a spoon rather than the knife. Peel the skin back or scoop the flesh out with a spoon, and slice it while it is still in its shell. Squeeze lemon or lime over it if it is going to sit, because cut avocado goes brown in the air."),
        (&["peel a mango", "cut a mango", "prepare a mango", "slice a mango", "how do i cut a mango"], "Slice the two cheeks off either side of the flat stone, score the flesh in a grid without cutting the skin, then push the skin inside out and cut the cubes off it. A peeler and a knife down the sides of the stone works too and wastes a little more. A ripe mango gives slightly under your thumb; a hard one is stringy and sour."),
        (&["cut up a pineapple", "cut a pineapple", "prepare a pineapple", "peel a pineapple", "how do i cut a pineapple", "how do i cut up a pineapple", "slice a pineapple"], "Slice the top and the base off flat so it stands up, then cut the skin away in strips from top to bottom, taking the eyes with it. Cut it into quarters, stand each one up and slice the core out, then cut the rest into the pieces you want. The core is tough raw but fine in a smoothie."),
        (&["juice a lemon", "juice a lime", "squeeze a lemon", "squeeze a lime", "juice an orange", "juice lemons", "squeeze lemons"], "Roll it on the worktop with the heel of your hand first to break the cells inside, then cut it across the middle rather than top to tail. Push a fork into the cut face and squeeze, turning as you go, and strain out the pips. A lemon at room temperature gives more juice than one out of the fridge."),
        (&["onions", "onion"], "Chop it, then soften it in oil on a low heat for 8 to 10 minutes with a pinch of salt: that is what makes it sweet and golden. A hot pan browns the outside and leaves the middle raw and sharp, so turn the heat down and give it the time."),
        (&["corn on the cob", "sweetcorn", "corn"], ""),
        (&["butternut squash", "butternut", "squash"], "Halve it, scoop out the seeds, rub the cut sides with oil and roast face down at 200C (180C fan) for 40 to 45 minutes. Cubed it takes 25 to 30 minutes, and it is done when a knife goes in with no resistance."),
        (&["pork chops", "pork chop"], "Pat them dry, season, and sear in a hot pan 3 to 4 minutes a side, then rest 5 minutes - pork is safe slightly pink now, at 70C in the middle. A thick chop also finishes in the oven at 200C (180C fan) for 10 to 12 minutes."),
        (&["fish", "white fish", "cod", "haddock", "salmon fillet", "fillet of fish"], "Bake fillets at 200C (180C fan) for 12 to 15 minutes - done when the thickest part flakes into opaque white, so keep it a minute short rather than a minute long - or fry them skin side down in a hot pan for 4 minutes, then 2 on the other side."),
        (&["potato wedges", "wedges"], "Cut them into wedges about 2cm at the thick edge, toss in oil, salt and paprika and roast at 200C (180C fan) for 25 to 30 minutes, turning once at halfway: they are done when the edges are deep gold and a knife slides into the thick part. Parboiling 8 minutes first makes them crisper outside and fluffier in."),
        (&["chard", "swiss chard"], "Cook the stems first: slice them and fry 4 to 5 minutes, then add the leaves for 2 to 3 minutes more, because the stems take far longer. A knob of butter, a pinch of salt and a squeeze of lemon, and the leaves wilt down to almost nothing."),
        (&["peppers", "bell peppers", "bell pepper"], "Fry sliced peppers in a hot pan with oil for 6 to 8 minutes until the edges soften and catch a little colour, or roast halves at 200C (180C fan) for 25 minutes. They go sweet with long cooking and stay crunchy with short cooking, so it depends what you want."),
        (&["celery", "celery sticks"], "Slice it and soften it in butter for 6 to 8 minutes: that is the base of a soup or a stew, and it goes sweet rather than stringy. On its own, braise the sticks with stock and a lid for 20 minutes, or shave them raw into a salad with lemon and salt."),
        (&["fennel", "fennel bulb"], "Slice it thin and roast at 200C (180C fan) for 25 minutes with oil, salt and a little lemon - the aniseed goes sweet. Raw and shaved with olive oil, lemon and parmesan it is a salad in 5 minutes. Cut out the tough core first."),
        (&["celeriac"], "Peel it thickly, cube it and boil for 15 to 20 minutes, then mash it half and half with potato - it is wet on its own and needs the potato to hold it. It browns fast once it is cut, so drop the pieces in water with a squeeze of lemon until they go in the pan; raw and shredded with mayonnaise, mustard and lemon it is remoulade."),
        (&["turnip", "turnips"], "Small young turnips only want 10 to 12 minutes boiled, or 25 minutes roasted in wedges at 200C (180C fan) with oil and honey. Big older ones are woody and bitter, so peel them well and mash them with potato and butter instead."),
        (&["broad beans", "fava beans"], "Boil them for 4 to 5 minutes, then pop them out of their skins - the skins are tough and bitter, and the beans inside are sweet. Small ones can stay in their skins. Dress them with oil, lemon, mint and a little salt."),
        (&["shallots", "shallot"], "Slice them and soften in butter for 6 to 8 minutes on a low heat - they are sweeter and milder than onions, so they need less time and brown faster. Whole, roast them at 200C (180C fan) for 25 to 30 minutes with oil and a pinch of salt."),
        (&["pak choi", "bok choy", "pak choy"], "Halve it lengthways and fry cut side down in a hot pan for 2 to 3 minutes, then add a splash of soy sauce, a little garlic and a lid for 1 minute more. It is done when the base is tender and the leaves have just wilted, so it takes about 5 minutes in all."),
        (&["mange tout", "mangetout", "sugar snap peas", "snow peas"], "Stir-fry them in a very hot pan for 2 to 3 minutes, or steam them for 3: they are done as soon as they are hot through and still squeak when you bite one. Don't boil them, or the sweetness goes into the water."),
        (&["buttermilk", "leftover buttermilk"], "Buttermilk is not cooked on its own: it is what makes soda bread rise with bread soda, it thins a pancake batter, and it tenderises chicken in a marinade for an hour before frying. It is naturally sour and thick, so in a recipe you can stand in for it with milk and a squeeze of lemon left for 10 minutes."),
        (&["marrow", "vegetable marrow", "marrows"], "Roast it in thick rings: peel it, scoop out the seeds, cut 2cm rings, oil and salt them and give them 30 to 35 minutes at 200C (180C fan) until the edges colour. Or halve it, salt it and let it drain 20 minutes, then stuff it with mince and rice and bake 45 minutes at 190C (170C fan) - it is mostly water, so the salt-and-drain is what stops it going to mush. If using minced meat, check that the centre reaches 70°C for at least two minutes."),
        (&["chicory", "endive"], "Halve it and braise cut side down in butter for 3 to 4 minutes, then add a splash of stock and a lid for 10 minutes, which takes the bitterness down. Raw, the leaves are a scoop for a dip, and a little sugar or orange in the pan helps too."),
    ];
