const SCIENCE_NEWTON_REPLIES: &[&str] = &["The first law says a thing keeps still, or keeps going in a straight line at the same speed, unless a force acts on it - which is why a passenger is thrown forward when a bus brakes, and why a seatbelt is the force that stops them. The second law is the useful one: force equals mass times acceleration, F = ma, so the same push moves a light thing quickly and a heavy one slowly, and a 1,000 kg car pushed with 2,000 N accelerates at 2 m/s each second. The third law says every force has an equal force back the other way: you push the ground and it pushes you forward, a rocket throws gas down and the gas throws the rocket up.", "The second law is the useful one: force equals mass times acceleration, F = ma, so the same push moves a light thing quickly and a heavy one slowly, and a 1,000 kg car pushed with 2,000 N accelerates at 2 m/s each second. The first law says a thing keeps still, or keeps going in a straight line at the same speed, unless a force acts on it - which is why a passenger is thrown forward when a bus brakes, and why a seatbelt is the force that stops them. The third law says every force has an equal force back the other way: you push the ground and it pushes you forward, a rocket throws gas down and the gas throws the rocket up.", "The third law says every force has an equal force back the other way: you push the ground and it pushes you forward, a rocket throws gas down and the gas throws the rocket up. The second law is the useful one: force equals mass times acceleration, F = ma, so the same push moves a light thing quickly and a heavy one slowly, and a 1,000 kg car pushed with 2,000 N accelerates at 2 m/s each second. The first law says a thing keeps still, or keeps going in a straight line at the same speed, unless a force acts on it - which is why a passenger is thrown forward when a bus brakes, and why a seatbelt is the force that stops them."];
