const COOKING_SCALE_UP: &str = "Double every ingredient and use a bigger tin rather than a deeper one, so the middle cooks at the same speed. It takes longer than twice the time is worth assuming: start checking at the original time and give it another ten or fifteen minutes if the skewer comes out wet. Salt, spice and raising agent go up by less than double, to taste, and a big batch is better split between two tins than crammed into one.";
const COOKING_SCALE_DOWN: &str = "Halve every ingredient and leave the oven temperature alone: the tin goes one size down if the batter looks thin in it, and start checking five minutes early. Cooking time does not halve - a smaller cake or loaf takes about two thirds as long - so test it with a skewer sooner than you think. Halve the raising agent exactly, and if the sum gives you half an egg, beat one and use half of it.";
const COOKING_PUDDING_IDEAS: &str = "An apple and blackberry crumble is the easiest crowd-pleaser: fruit in a dish, a crumble of butter, flour and sugar on top, 35 minutes at 180C, and custard. Baked rice pudding and bread and butter pudding are just as easy and soft enough for anyone who finds chewing hard; Eton mess takes five minutes if you buy the meringues.";
const COOKING_KIDS_VEG: &str = "Keep serving one small piece of veg next to something they already like, without a fight or a reward: it can take ten tries before a toddler accepts a new one. Roasting carrots or broccoli in a little oil makes them sweeter, and a dip to dunk turns it into a game. Grated or blended into what they already eat - carrot in the bolognese, courgette in a muffin, spinach in a soup - gets the veg in without a standoff.";
const COOKING_ADULT_VEG: &str = "Put them where you already eat rather than on a separate pile: grated carrot or courgette in the mince, a handful of spinach stirred into pasta at the end, frozen peas straight into rice, beans or lentils bulking out a chilli. Frozen and tinned count, and so does a bag of mixed veg thrown into a tray bake. Aim for one extra portion at a meal you already have rather than a new meal you have to keep up.";
const COOKING_DEFINITION_WORDS: &[&str] = &["mean", "means", "meaning", "difference", "what is", "what's", "whats", "between", "versus", "vs", "definition"];
