const NATURE_DAYLIGHT_WINTER: &str = "Daylight hours swing with the Earth's tilt: your half leans away from the Sun in winter, so it rises later, climbs less high and sets earlier, and the day is short. In the UK that is about 7 hours 45 minutes of daylight around 21 December, against 16 hours 45 minutes in June.";
const NATURE_DAYLIGHT_SUMMER: &str = "The same tilt the other way: your half leans towards the Sun in summer, so it rises earlier, climbs higher and sets later, and the day is long. In the UK that is about 16 hours 45 minutes of daylight around 21 June, against 7 hours 45 minutes in December.";
const NATURE_DAYLIGHT_SCOTLAND: &str = " In the far north of Scotland around midsummer the sun only dips just below the horizon, so it never gets properly dark - the simmer dim - and Shetland's longest day is over 18 hours of light.";
