const PLANS_FREE_WEEKEND: &str = "Pick two things and put them in the diary by Friday: one that gets you out of the house - a walk with a destination, a market on Saturday morning, a museum or gallery you keep meaning to visit - and one person to see or one thing waiting for you at home. The free ones that work are the walk, the park, a canal, a hill with a view, the library and a market on a Saturday morning. Two plans makes a good weekend; five makes a list you abandon by Saturday lunchtime. An evening is the same rule: one thing out and one thing in. Tell me the town and who is with you and I can suggest something specific.";
const PLANS_DAY_OFF_PLAN: &str = "Treat it as a small Saturday: one thing out of the house (a walk you have not done, a market, a museum or gallery - most are free), one job you keep putting off that takes an hour, and one person to see. Put times on the first two the night before; a free day goes in small pieces when nothing is decided.";
const PLANS_EVENING_PLAN: &str = "Give the evening a shape rather than waiting for an idea: one thing you will actually do at 8pm (a film you have been meaning to watch, a walk before it gets dark, a call to someone you like), tea that takes a bit of effort, and the phone in another room for the first hour. A free evening feels long when nothing is decided and short when one thing is.";
const PLANS_TRY_ONCE: &[&str] = &["Learn to make one loaf of bread by hand: flour, water, yeast, salt, an hour of rising, and the house does the rest. The first one is always a bit wonky and nobody minds.", "Try an outdoor swim at a supervised lido or organised beginner session with a friend. Follow the venue's guidance on water temperature and conditions.", "See a band you have never heard of in a small room on a week night. A cheap ticket, no expectations, and it is the best way to find music you keep.", "Take one night away on your own: a train, a cheap room, a book. It is a different kind of trip from a holiday with people, and easier than it sounds.", "Watch the sunrise on purpose once: check the time, set an alarm, take a flask. One early start buys you a morning you will talk about for years.", "Learn to change a tyre and check your oil before you have to. Ten minutes of practice on your own drive beats an hour on a hard shoulder in the rain.", "Eat in a restaurant by yourself, with a book, once. Nobody is looking, and it is the most peaceful hour you can buy for the price of a lunch.", "Post a real letter to somebody who will not expect it - a grandparent, an old teacher, a friend who moved away. It lands differently from a message.", "Plant something you can eat and keep it alive to the plate. Herbs on a windowsill count, and one tomato plant teaches you more than a book does.", "Go to a match of a sport you know nothing about and sit with the crowd. Someone will explain the rules before half time, and the noise is the point.", "Give a morning to something local - a food bank, a park clean-up, a repair cafe. One morning with no commitment afterwards, and you will meet people you would never otherwise meet.", "Cook the same dish once a week until you can do it without the recipe. Everyone should own one dinner, and six goes is usually enough.", "Draw something badly on purpose, and keep it. Being a beginner at something for an afternoon is good for you.", "Catch a train to a town an hour away with no plan for the day: walk it, have lunch, come home. The having-no-plan is the good bit.", "Take a class where you make something with your hands - pottery, woodwork, knitting, welding. A thing that stays made is a rare sort of satisfaction.", "Sleep outside once: a tent in a friend's garden, a campsite, a bothy. You will remember the night, not how well you slept.", "Learn ten words of a language before you go somewhere that speaks it. Shopkeepers forgive a lot for one honest attempt.", "Ask an older relative to tell you about their childhood and record it on your phone. It is the cheapest history you will ever collect."];
