const LEISURE_NEWBORN_WEEK: &str = "With a newborn the week plans itself, so plan the small things instead: take the nights in shifts so one of you gets a four-hour stretch, get out once a day even if it is only round the block, and say yes to anyone who offers to hold the baby while you sleep. Register the birth inside 42 days, keep the health visitor's number to hand, and book nothing that has a start time - a week off now is recovery, not a holiday.";
const LEISURE_DAY_PLAN: &str = "Put two things in the afternoon and give each a time: one that gets you out (a walk you have not done, a park, the library) and one you keep putting off that takes an hour. A free afternoon with no time on it is what dissolves into the phone.";
const LEISURE_DAY_OFF_PLAN: &str = "Treat it as a small Saturday: one thing out of the house (a walk you have not done, a market, a museum or gallery - most are free), one job you keep putting off that takes an hour, and one person to see. Put times on the first two the night before; a free day goes in small pieces when nothing is decided.";
const LEISURE_EVENING_PLAN: &str = "Give the evening a shape rather than waiting for an idea: one thing you will actually do at 8pm (a film you have been meaning to watch, a walk before it gets dark, a call to someone you like), tea that takes a bit of effort, and the phone in another room for the first hour. A free evening feels long when nothing is decided and short when one thing is.";
const LEISURE_WEEK_PLAN: &str = "Put a couple of nights away in the middle of the week and keep the first and last days free: somewhere two or three hours away by train is enough, and going Tuesday to Thursday costs less than the weekend. Fill the other days a day at a time - one thing out of the house, one job you keep putting off - and a week off stops slipping past unnoticed.";
const LEISURE_WEEKEND_PLAN: &str = "Three things, one each: something out of the house (a walk you have not done, a park, the library, a free museum), one job you keep putting off but that takes an hour (the drawer, the bike, the photos), and one thing with a person - a call, a walk with someone, a coffee. Put the outdoor one on the better day and keep Sunday evening free, or the weekend ends in a rush and feels like it never happened.";
const LEISURE_WEEK_PLAN_SKINT: &str = "Start with the days that cost nothing: the walk you have not done, the library, a market, a park with a flask and a sandwich. Put the one paid thing in the middle of the week, because a cheap train day trip on a Tuesday off-peak is the best value going, and give each day one job out of the house so the week does not go by in small pieces.";
const LEISURE_SUMMER_WEEK: &str = "In summer, put the outdoors before ten or after five and keep the middle of the day for the job you keep putting off, so the heat sets the day instead of you.";
const LEISURE_WINTER_WEEK: &str = "In winter, plan round the dark: the walk or the market at midday, and the cinema, the museum and the long cook for the evenings.";
const LEISURE_NO_MONEY: &str = "None of that has to cost anything: the walk and the library are free either way, and a flask beats a cafe.";
