    pub(super) const CANCEL_WINDOW: &str = "Anything bought online has 14 days from delivery to change your mind, whatever the shop's own page says, and another 14 days to send it back.";
    pub(super) const CANCEL_AT_WAREHOUSE: &str = "Cancel it on your account page or by ringing with the order number, and ask for the cancellation in writing - a cancelled order that still ships is the commonest version of this.";
    pub(super) const CANCEL_REFUSE: &str = "If the parcel comes anyway, refuse the delivery rather than opening it.";
    pub(super) const CANCEL_REFUND: &str = "If they will not refund, a card chargeback or Section 75 through your bank is the next step.";
    pub(super) const TENANCY_DEFINITION: &str = "A tenancy agreement is the contract between you and the landlord: the rent and what it includes, how long the tenancy runs, the notice each side gives, the deposit and which protection scheme holds it, and who repairs what. Read it before you sign, especially the break clause and any fees, and keep your copy - your landlord has to give you one, along with the deposit protection certificate, the gas safety certificate and the energy performance certificate.";
    pub(super) const STOLEN_BAG: &str = "Cancel and block first, in this order: freeze every card in your bank's app, ask your network to bar the phone, then report it to the police on 101 for a crime reference number, which your insurance will ask for. If your keys and address were both in the bag, get the locks changed rather than hoping, and tell the bank you are worried about your address too. Most home insurance covers a bag taken away from home, and most policies want to hear within a few days.";
    pub(super) const STOLEN_BAG_AWAY: &str = "Report it to the police where you are and get a written report - a copy in English if you can - because your travel insurer and any replacement passport both ask for one. Then freeze the cards in your bank's app, have your network bar the phone, and ring your embassy or consulate if the passport went with it. Take photos of the report on your phone; paper gets lost.";
    pub(super) const STOLEN_THING_GEAR: &str = "Report it on 101 and keep the crime reference number, then ring your home contents insurer: an item taken from the house, the car or a bag is usually covered, and the claim wants the serial number or a photo and the receipt. For a laptop, camera or watch, sign out of the accounts on it remotely and change the password on your main email, because that is the key to everything else. If it was taken in a burglary, do not tidy up before the police or the insurer have seen it.";
    pub(super) const PASSPORT_CLAIM: &str = "Report it to HM Passport Office as soon as you know it is gone, so it is cancelled and cannot be used, and report it to the police as well - keep the crime reference. If you need to travel soon, ask the Passport Office about an emergency travel document or the fast-track service rather than waiting for a new one. The carrier's claim covers only what the posting cost, not the passport fee, so start the replacement the same day.";
    pub(super) const PARCEL_CLAIM: &str = "Find the proof of posting - the receipt from the counter or the drop-off, or the emailed label and its barcode - because that is what a claim rests on. Then open a claim with the carrier you handed it to, not the shop: Royal Mail keeps a lost item claim open from 10 working days after posting up to 80 days, and pays what the service covers - about £20 on standard with proof of posting, £50 on Signed For, more on Special Delivery, so check the cover before you count on it. Put the value and receipts in the claim, and if the item was tracked, add the tracking number to it. A card or a letter goes the same way.";
    pub(super) const PASSPORT_PHOTO_PRICE: &str = "About £5 to £8 for a strip of four from a booth, and £8 to £12 in a chemist, a camera shop or a Post Office, where it is checked as it is taken. A digital code for an online application is usually included; a passport office's own service costs more again because the fee is for the check.";
    pub(super) const PASSPORT_PHOTO_PLACES: &str = "A photo booth is the quickest: about £5 to £8 for a strip, and most are in a supermarket, a station or a big chemist. A chemist, a camera shop such as Snappy Snaps or Timpson, or a Post Office takes them for £8 to £12 and checks the photo as it goes, which is worth it for a baby. Whichever you use, the photo still has to be a plain light background, no glasses, mouth closed, taken in the last month.";
    pub(super) const PASSPORT_PHOTO_AT_HOME: &str = "Yes - for an online application a phone photo is fine and it is what most people do: stand them against a plain light wall in daylight, so the background is bare, with no flash and no shadow, get someone else to take it from about arm's length at the height of the face, keep the shoulders in, and crop it square. The form tells you whether it passes before you pay, so take a few and use the best.";
    pub(super) const PASSPORT_PHOTO_RULES: &str = "The photo is the part that gets an application sent back: plain light background, no glasses, mouth closed and a neutral face, taken in the last month, with no shadow on the face. An online form wants a square digital file rather than a photo of a print, and the size rule is on your government's site.";
